(window.MAG=window.MAG||{}).d=(window.MAG.d||{});window.MAG.d["2-lateran-v"]=[["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p1","Fifth Lateran Council A.d. 1512–1517","Julius, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Iulius episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p2","Fifth Lateran Council A.d. 1512–1517","Since we intend to prosecute, the right hand of the Most High assisting, the celebration of this sacred Lateran council already begun for the praise of God, the peace of the universal church, the union of the faithful, the overthrow of schism and heresies, the reformation of morals, and the expedition against the perfidious enemies of the faith, so that the mouths of all schismatics and enemies of peace, like barking dogs, may be stopped, and all Christ's faithful may be able to keep themselves immaculate from such pestiferous and venomous contagion—in this present second session, legitimately gathered in the Holy Spirit, concerning the indiction, convocation, and publication of the schismatic conventicle and asserted conciliabulum of Pisa, issued for the purpose of dividing and scandalizing the union of the aforesaid church, [concerning] the citations, monitions, decrees, absolutions, sentences, acts, creations of legates, subtractions of obediences, asserted censures, appositions, and the translation of the same conciliabulum to the cities of Milan or Vercelli or any other places whatsoever, and the reprobations, condemnations, revocations, cassations, irritations, and annullments of all and singular things done and concluded in the said conciliabulum, after having held mature deliberation with our venerable brothers the cardinals of the holy Roman church, and by the counsel and unanimous consent of the same brothers, and from certain knowledge and from the plenitude of apostolic power, by our various letters respectively—especially issued under the date of the 15th of the Kalends of August [July 18] in the eighth year of our pontificate, and the 3rd of the Nones of December [December 3] and the Ides of April [April 13] in the ninth year of the same pontificate—made and emanated, and the same letters with the decrees, declarations, inhibitions, mandates, exhortations, monitions, appositions of ecclesiastical interdicts and other sentences, censures, and penalties, as well from the canonical sanctions as from us especially in the letters of indiction of this sacred universal council, and all and singular other clauses contained in the said letters, the tenors of which we wish to be held as expressed as if they were inserted word for word into the presents, although as firm and valid they needed no other confirmation or approval—for greater caution and manifestation of truth, with this sacred council approving, we confirm, approve, and renew them, and we wish, decree, and ordain them to be observed inviolably, supplying all and singular defects, if any perchance have intervened in them.","Cum inchoatam huius sacri Lateranensis concilii celebrationem ad dei laudem universalis ecclesiae pacem fidelium unionem schismatis et haeresum eversionem morum reformationem ac contra perfidos fidei hostes expeditionem altissimi dextera assistente prosequi intendamus ut omnium schismaticorum et pacis hostium latrantium canum ora obtundantur universi que christi fideles valeant a tam pestifero et venenoso contagio se immaculatos servare in hac praesenti secunda sessione in spiritu sancto legitime congregata indictionis convocationis et publicationis schismaticae conventiculae et asserti conciliabuli Pisani ad scindendum et scandalizandum ecclesiae praefatae unionem emanatarum citationum monitionum decretorum absolutionum sententiarum actorum legatorum creationum obedientiarum subtractionum assertarum censurarum appositionum ipsius que conciliabuli translationis ad Mediolanum vel Vercellensem civitates aut alia loca quaecumque omnium que et singulorum in dicto conciliabulo gestorum et conclusorum reprobationes damnationes revocationes cassationes irritationes et annullationes per nos habita cum venerabilibus fratribus nostris sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalibus deliberatione matura et de eorumdem fratrum consilio et unanimi consensu ac ex certa scientia et de apostolicae potestatis plenitudine per diversas nostras literas respective praesertim sub data quintodecimo kalendas augusti pontificatus nostri anno octavo ac tertio nonas decembris et idibus aprilis eiusdem pontificatus anno nono editas factas et emanatas literas que ipsas cum decretis declarationibus inhibitionibus mandatis hortationibus monitionibus interdictorum ecclesiasticorum appositionibus et aliis sententiis censuris et poenis tam a canonicis sanctionibus quam a nobis maxime in literis indictionis huius sacri universalis concilii ac omnibus et singulis aliis clausulis in dictis literis contentis quarum tenores ac si de verbo ad verbum praesentibus insererentur pro expressis haberi volumus licet ut firma et valida nulla alia confirmatione aut approbatione egerent ad abundantiorem cautelam et veritatis manifestationem hoc sacro approbante concilio confirmamus approbamus et innovamus illa que inviolabiliter observari volumus statuimus et ordinamus supplentes omnes et singulos defectus si qui forsan intervenerint in eisdem."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p3","Fifth Lateran Council A.d. 1512–1517","And likewise we condemn and reprobate the aforesaid conciliabulum, its translation, and all and singular things done, made, done, and published, or ordained by the same conciliabulum and by those assisting, adhering, favoring, and consenting to it, directly or indirectly, in any way and manner whatsoever, from the day of the indiction of the same conciliabulum up to the present day, and to be done, carried out, made, and published in the future—even if they are or were such of which special, specific, express, and individual mention would have to be had, holding their tenors and qualities also as expressed—as other adulterine councils deviating from the truth and the things done in them were condemned and reprobated by law and by the sacred canons; and we announce that they have been and are null, void, and empty, of no strength or moment; and insofar as it is necessary, we quash, make void, and annul them, and we wish them to be held as quashed, made void, and annulled.","Nec non conciliabulum praefatum illius que translationem ac omnia et singula per ipsum conciliabulum illi que assistentes adhaerentes fautores et consentientes directe vel indirecte quomodocumque et qualitercumque a die indictionis ipsius conciliabuli usque in praesentem diem acta facta gesta et prolata ac in posterum gerenda agenda facienda et proferenda etiamsi talia sint vel fuerint de quibus specialis specifica expressa ac individua mentio habenda foret illorum tenores et qualitates etiam pro expressis habentes ut alia adulterina et a veritate deviantia concilia et in eis gesta a iure sacris que canonibus damnata et reprobata fuerunt damnamus et reprobamus ac prout sunt nulla irrita et inania nullius que roboris vel momenti fuisse et esse nuntiamus et quatenus opus sit cassamus irritamus et annullamus ac pro cassis irritis et annullatis haberi volumus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p4","Fifth Lateran Council A.d. 1512–1517","And nonetheless, with this same sacred council approving, we decree and declare that this sacred ecumenical council, justly, reasonably, and for true and legitimate causes, duly and rightly indicted, begun to be celebrated, and all and singular things which have been done and done in it and shall be done and carried out hereafter, are just, reasonable, firm, and valid, and that it has and obtains the same strength, the same force, power, authority, and firmness which other general councils—especially the Lateran [Council] approved by the sacred canons—have and obtain.","Et nihilo minus hoc sacrum concilium oecumenicum iuste rationabiliter ac ex veris et legitimis causis rite que et recte indictum celebrari inceptum omnia que et singula quae in eo facta et gesta sunt et de cetero fient ac gerentur iusta rationabilia firma et valida fore ac idem robur eamdem que vim potestatem auctoritatem et firmitatem habere et obtinere quae alia concilia generalia praesertim Lateranense a sacris canonibus approbata habent et obtinent hoc eodem sacro concilio approbante decernimus et declaramus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p5","Fifth Lateran Council A.d. 1512–1517","Moreover, since the disposition of times and the summer heat pressing, so that the convenience and health of the prelates may be consulted, and those from beyond the mountains and from across the sea who have not been able to come up to now to this sacred council may be awaited, on account of which and other just and reasonable causes known to the aforesaid sacred council and approved by it, with this aforesaid sacred council likewise approving, we indict the third session of the continuation of the celebration of the aforesaid council to be made on the 3rd of the Nones of November [November 3] next to come.","Insuper que temporum dispositione aestivis que caloribus instantibus ut commoditati et valetudini praelatorum consulatur ultramontani que et trans mare existentes qui hactenus ad hoc sacrum concilium venire non potuerunt expectentur propter que alias iustas et rationabiles causas praefato sacro concilio notas et ab eo approbatas tertiam sessionem continuationis celebrationis concilii praefati tertio nonas novembris proxime futuri faciendam hoc praefato sacro concilio similiter approbante indicimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p6","Fifth Lateran Council A.d. 1512–1517","We also grant and indulge, with the aforesaid sacred council also approving, license and faculty to all and singular prelates and others then existing in the same council to depart from the Roman curia and to stay wherever they please, provided that, a legitimate impediment of which clear proof exists ceasing, they be present on the said 3rd of the Nones of November [November 3] in the aforesaid Lateran council, under the incurrence of the penalties in the letters of indiction of the same council and in the canonical sanctions enacted against those not coming to councils.","Omnibus que et singulis praelatis et aliis in eodem concilio tunc existentibus a Romana curia recedendi et pro eorum voluntate ubi eis placuerit morandi dummodo dicta tertio nonas novembris in praefato concilio Lateranensi cessante legitimo impedimento de quo clare constet sub poenarum in literis indictionis ipsius concilii et canonicis sanctionibus contra non accedentes ad concilia indictarum incursu praesentes existant licentiam et facultatem praefato sacro concilio etiam approbante concedimus et indulgimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p7","Fifth Lateran Council A.d. 1512–1517","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p8","Session II (july 17, 1512)","Julius, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Iulius episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p9","Session II (july 17, 1512)","Prosecuting the sacred Lateran council for the praise and glory of Him whose works are perfect, in this third session legitimately gathered with the Holy Spirit favoring—which we had indicted in the second session to be made on the 3rd of the Nones of November just past, and afterwards, by the counsel and unanimous assent of our venerable brothers the cardinals of the holy Roman church, from certain causes then expressed and other legitimate causes, we prorogued and indicted it to be held on this present day, the same sacred council approving such prorogation and indiction on account of the aforesaid causes known to it—after the happy and fortunate true adherence and sincere union of our dearest son in Christ, Maximilian, elected emperor of the Romans, ever august, to this sacrosanct Lateran council, all and singular things done, done, made, written, and published, or ordained by the pupils of damnation, Bernardinus Carvajal, Guillelmus Brizonet, Renatus de Pria, and Fridericus de Sancto Severino, formerly cardinals, and their favorers, adherents, accomplices, and followers, schismatics, heretics, and raging to their own and others' perdition, striving to divide the unity of holy mother church in the conciliabulum of Pisa, Milan, Lyons, or elsewhere—whatever, how many, and of whatever kind they may be, even the impositions of tithes in the whole kingdom of France made by them, the tenors and qualities of which we wish to be held as expressed as if inserted word for word into the presents, not however through general clauses—up to this present day, and perhaps to be done in the future, although they are null, void, and empty, and have been condemned and reprobated by us with the aforesaid sacred council approving, adhering for greater caution to such condemnation and reprobation, with the same sacred council approving, we condemn, reprobate, and detest them, and we decree and declare that they have been and are null, void, and empty, of no strength, efficacy, effect, or moment.","Ad illius cuius perfecta sunt opera laudem et gloriam sacrum Lateranense concilium prosequentes in hac tertia sessione spiritu sancto favente legitime congregata quam alias tertio nonas novembris proxime praeteriti faciendam in secunda sessione indixeramus et deinde de venerabilium fratrum nostrorum sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalium consilio unanimi que assensu in hodiernum diem tenendam ex certis tunc expressis et aliis legitimis causis prorogavimus et indiximus eodem sacro concilio prorogationem et indictionem huiusmodi ex causis praefatis sibi notis approbante post felicem faustam que carissimi in christo filii nostri Maximiliani Romanorum imperatoris electi semper augusti huic sacrosancto Lateranensi concilio veram adhaesionem et sinceram unionem omnia et singula per damnationis alumnos Bernardinum Carvaial Guillelmum Brizonet Renatum de Pria et Fridericum de sancto Severino olim cardinales eorum que fautores adhaerentes complices et sequaces schismaticos haereticos et in suam ac aliorum perniciem debacchantes unitatem sanctae matris ecclesiae scindere satagentes in conciliabulo Pisano Mediolani Lugduni vel alibi quaecumque quotcumque et qualiacumque fuerint etiam decimarum impositiones in universo regno Franciae per eos factas quorum tenores et qualitates ac si praesentibus de verbo ad verbum non autem per clausulas generales insererentur pro expressis haberi volumus usque in praesentem diem acta gesta et facta conscripta et publicata seu ordinata et in futurum forsitan gerenda quamvis sint nulla irrita et inania et a nobis praefato concilio sacro approbante damnata et reprobata fuerint potiori pro cautela damnationi et reprobationi huiusmodi inhaerendo eodem sacro concilio approbante damnamus reprobamus et detestamur illa que nulla irrita et inania nullius que roboris efficaciae effectus vel momenti fuisse et esse decernimus et declaramus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p10","Session II (july 17, 1512)","And also our letters by which, on account of the receptions, favors, adherence, and aid notoriously given by the king of France and several other prelates, officials, nobles, and barons of the kingdom of France to schismatics and heretics in the prosecution of the said condemned and reprobated conciliabulum of Pisa, we subjected the kingdom of France and especially Lyons—the duchy of Brittany excepted—to ecclesiastical interdict under date namely at Rome, at St. Peter's, in the year of the Lord's incarnation 1512, the Ides of August [August 13], in the ninth year of our pontificate, by the counsel of the aforesaid brothers, and inhibited the fairs accustomed to be held at Lyons from being held thereafter at Lyons, and transferred the said fairs to be made at the city of Geneva, with the decrees, declarations, inhibitions, and all and singular clauses contained in them, similarly with the aforesaid sacred council having full knowledge of them and approving, we renew, and we subject the aforesaid kingdom as aforesaid, and its cities, lands, towns, and any places whatsoever to such interdict, and we transfer the same fairs from Lyons to the aforesaid city of Geneva to be made.","Nec non literas nostras per quas propter receptiones favores adhaerentiam et auxilium per regem Franciae et nonnullos alios regni Franciae praelatos officiales nobiles et barones schismaticis et haereticis in dicti conciliabuli Pisani damnati et reprobati prosecutione notorie praestita regnum Franciae et praesertim Lugdunum ducatu Britanniae excepto sub data videlicet Romae apud sanctum Petrum anno incarnationis dominicae millesimo quingentesimo duodecimo idus augusti pontificatus nostri anno nono de fratrum praedictorum consilio ecclesiastico subiecimus interdicto nundinas que Lugduni fieri solitas in Lugduno extunc de cetero fieri inhibuimus dictas que nundinas in civitate Gebennensi faciendas transtulimus cum decretis declarationibus inhibitionibus omnibus que et singulis in eis contentis clausulis similiter sacro concilio praedicto de illis plenariam notitiam habente ac approbante innovamus regnum que praefatum ut praefertur illius que civitates terras oppida et loca quaecumque huiusmodi interdicto subiicimus ac ipsas nundinas ex Lugduno in civitatem praefatam Gebennensem faciendas transferimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p11","Session II (july 17, 1512)","And so that this sacred Lateran council may be brought to the fruit of most abundant fertility, and many other and grave matters in this sacred Lateran council come to be treated and discussed for the praise of omnipotent God and the exaltation of the universal church, with the aforesaid sacred council approving all things, we indict the fourth session of the continuation of its celebration to be made on the 4th of the Ides of the present month of December [December 10].","Et ut sacrum hoc Lateranense concilium ad finem uberrimae fertilitatis deducatur alia que plura et gravia in hoc sacro Lateranensi concilio tractanda et discutienda veniant ad dei omnipotentis laudem et ecclesiae universalis exaltationem quartam sessionem continuationis celebrationis ipsius quarto idus praesentis mensis decembris faciendam sacro concilio praefato omnia approbante indicimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p12","Session II (july 17, 1512)","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p13","Session III (december 10, 1512)","Julius, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Iulius episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p14","Session III (december 10, 1512)","Intending with paternal and solicitous studies for the salvation of the flock committed to us from above, for the reformation of morals, and for the defense of ecclesiastical liberty and the tranquillity and increase of the catholic faith, for the praise and glory of omnipotent God and the individual Trinity, with this sacred council approving, we approve and renew our letters long ago emanated by us, known to the same council, by which we generally reformed the officials of the Roman curia and their exactions; and we command and enjoin that those [letters] which afterwards we mandated to be declared by certain persons then expressed to the utility of the faithful according to our intention—through the aforesaid expressed persons, and together with them other prelates of various nations now present in the aforesaid council to be deputed, all things which could pervert human judgment ceasing, as shall be just and equitable—be particularly declared, and that the declarations themselves be referred to us in this sacred council in other sessions and approved by the same council, so that they may be duly executed.","Saluti gregis nobis desuper commissi morum reformationi defensioni que ecclesiasticae libertatis ac fidei catholicae tranquillitati et augmento paternis et solicitis studiis intendentes ad omnipotentis dei et individuae trinitatis laudem et gloriam sacro hoc approbante concilio literas nostras a nobis dudum emanatas eidem concilio notas per quas Romanae curiae officiales et illorum exactiones generaliter reformavimus approbamus et innovamus illas que quas per nonnullos postea tunc expressos ad fidelium utilitatem iuxta intentionem nostram declarari mandavimus per praefatos expressos et cum illis alios diversarum nationum praelatos nunc in praefato concilio praesentes deputandos omnibus quae iudicium humanum pervertere possent cessantibus ut iustum et aequum fuerit particulariter declarari et declarationes ipsas in aliis sessionibus nobis in hoc sacro concilio referendas et ab eodem concilio approbandas ut debite exequantur praecipimus et mandamus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p15","Session III (december 10, 1512)","Moreover, since for many periods of time, by several prelates of the Gallic nation and noble laypersons and others favoring them, especially under pretext of a certain sanction which they themselves call the Pragmatica [Sanction], there has been enormous detraction from the apostolic see and from the holy Roman church, the head of the universal church, its liberty and authority, and derogation from the sacred canons—we, being no longer able to tolerate so pernicious a thing, in offense of God and contempt and evident detriment of the aforesaid church, with this sacred council approving, commit the negotiation and discussion of such declaration and abrogation to be made by us to the congregations of our venerable brothers the cardinals of the aforesaid church and of other prelates, which we shall cause to be made in the upper Lateran hall as often as necessary, and to make a relation of the things discussed in the first or other sessions as may be done conveniently to us and the sacred council—the same sacred council approving.","Insuper cum iam per multa temporum spatia per nonnullos praelatos Gallicae nationis et nobiles laicos ac alios illis faventes praesertim cuiusdam sanctionis praetextu quam ipsi pragmaticam vocant apostolicae sedi sanctae que Romanae universalis ecclesiae capiti libertati et auctoritati enormiter detractum sacris que canonibus derogatum fuerit nos rem adeo perniciosam in dei offensam et ecclesiae praefatae vilipendium et evidens detrimentum amplius ferre nequeuntes cum sanctio ipsa ab omni ad id legitima potestate carentibus nullorum que summorum pontificum aut generalium legitimorum conciliorum auctoritate facta sed quodam abusu tantum in illis partibus introducta et observata fuerit illa que cum in ea contentis nulla et invalida merito declarari et abrogari debeat quam etiam clarae memoriae Ludovicus xi Francorum rex abrogavit ut in eius patentibus literis desuper confectis plenius continetur. Declarationis igitur et abrogationis per nos faciendarum huiusmodi negotium et discussionem venerabilium fratrum nostrorum ecclesiae praefatae cardinalium et aliorum praelatorum congregationibus quas in Lateranensi sala superiori quatenus opus fuerit facient relationem que discussorum in prima aut aliis sessionibus prout commode fieri poterit nobis et sacro concilio faciendam eodem sacro concilio approbante committimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p16","Session III (december 10, 1512)","We decree and ordain that the Gallic prelates, chapters of churches and monasteries, parliaments, and laypersons favoring them—abusing the aforesaid sanction or approving it, of whatever dignity, even if they be of royal dignity—and all and singular others thinking that their own interest is concerned collectively or individually, by public edict (since safe access to those parts is not open) to be affixed to the doors of the churches of Milan, Asti, and Pavia, be warned and cited to appear before us and the aforesaid council within a certain suitable term to be fixed, and to say why the aforesaid sanction, its corruption and abuse in matters concerning the authority, dignity, and unity of the Roman church and the apostolic see and the violation of the sacred canons and ecclesiastical liberty, ought not to be declared, decreed, and abrogated as null and void; and we decree and ordain that those thus warned and cited are bound and held just as if they had been warned and cited personally.","Gallicos que praelatos capitula ecclesiarum et monasteriorum parlamenta et laicos illis faventes cuiuscumque dignitatis etiam si regalis existerent sanctione praefata abutentes seu illam approbantes omnes que et singulos alios sua communiter vel divisim interesse putantes per edictum publicum cum ad partes illas tutus non pateat accessus in Mediolanensis Astensis et Papiensis ecclesiarum valvis affigendum moneri et citari infra certum competentem terminum praefigendum ad comparendum coram nobis et concilio praefato causas que dicendum quare sanctio praefata illius que corruptela et abusio in concernentibus auctoritatem dignitatem et unitatem ecclesiae Romanae et apostolicae sedis sacrorum que canonum et ecclesiasticae libertatis violationem nulla et invalida declarari decerni et abrogari non debeat illos que sic monitos et citatos arctari et teneri ac si personaliter moniti et citati fuissent statuimus et decernimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p17","Session III (december 10, 1512)","Furthermore, with the said sacred council approving, we decree that all and singular provisions, collations of ecclesiastical benefices, confirmations of elections and postulations, concessions, graces, mandates, and indults whatsoever, concerning both grace and justice or mixed, of whatever tenor they may exist—which we wish to be held as expressed in the presents—made and granted by the synagogue or conciliabulum of Pisa and its schismatic adherents, lacking all authority and dignities, although they are null and void, yet for greater caution are null and void and of no strength or moment; and that all and singular persons, of whatever dignity, state, grade, nobility, order, and condition they may be, to whom they were granted or whose benefit, utility, or honor they regard in any way, do not make their fruits, rents, and proceeds nor have made them, but are held to their restitution and to the relinquishment of the same benefices and other things granted; and unless they shall have actually and completely relinquished those benefices and other things granted to them within two months from the date of the presents, they are ipso facto deprived even of other ecclesiastical benefices which they possess by just title—with the said sacred council approving.","Ulterius omnes et singulas provisiones collationes beneficiorum ecclesiasticorum confirmationes electionum et postulationum concessionum gratias mandata et indulta quaecumque tam gratiam quam iustitiam seu mixtim concernentia cuiuscumque tenoris existant quae praesentibus pro expressis haberi volumus per synagogam vel conciliabulum Pisanum illius que schismaticos adhaerentes auctoritate et dignitatibus omnibus carentes facta et concessa quamvis sint nulla et invalida pro potiori tamen cautela nulla et irrita nullius que roboris vel momenti esse omnes que et singulos cuiuscumque dignitatis status gradus nobilitatis ordinis et conditionis existentes quibus concessa fuerunt aut quorum commodum utilitatem seu honorem quomodolibet respiciunt nullos eorum fructus redditus et proventus suos facere vel hactenus fecisse sed ad illorum restitutionem et ipsorum beneficiorum et aliorum praemissorum concessorum dimissionem teneri et nisi beneficia ipsa et alia eis concessa infra duos menses a data praesentium computandos realiter et omnino dimiserint etiam aliis beneficiis ecclesiasticis quae iusto titulo possident eo ipso privatos existere dicto sacro approbante concilio decernimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p18","Session III (december 10, 1512)","We also apply the fruits, rents, and proceeds of this kind, and any moneys of the tithes imposed by the said conciliabulum, exacted and to be exacted, to the expedition to be made against the infidels.","Fructus quoque redditus et proventus huiusmodi ac quasvis pecunias decimarum per dictum conciliabulum impositarum exactos et exigendos expeditioni contra infideles faciendae applicamus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p19","Session III (december 10, 1512)","So that the declaration of the reformation and nullity of the aforesaid sanction and other things to be done may be maturely executed, and the prelates about to come to this sacred council—some of whom, we have heard, have already taken up the journey to come—may be able to come conveniently, with the aforesaid sacred council approving, we indict the fifth session to be made on the 14th of the Kalends of March [February 16], which will be the fourth feria after the first Sunday of the coming Lent.","Ut autem declaratio reformationis et nullitatis sanctionis praedictae alia que gerenda mature exequi et praelati ad sacrum concilium huiusmodi venturi quorum aliquos iam iter ad veniendum arripuisse accepimus commode venire possint quintam sessionem decimoquarto kalendas martii quae erit quarta feria post primam dominicam quadragesimae proxime futurae faciendam praefato sacro concilio approbante indicimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p20","Session III (december 10, 1512)","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p21","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis on the Reformation of Cardinals and Clergy","Leo, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for future remembrance of the matter.","Leo episcopus servus servorum dei ad futuram rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p22","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis on the Reformation of Cardinals and Clergy","By the ordinance of Him who governs heavenly and earthly things by omnipotent providence, presiding although unworthily over His holy universal church, among the manifold cares by which we are continually anguished, admonished by the salutary and most holy doctrine of the Doctor of the Gentiles, we turn the point of our mind especially to those things through which perpetual unity and inviolate charity may endure in the church itself, the flock committed to us may proceed by straight paths into the way of salvation, and the name of Christians and the sign of the precious cross—in which the faithful have been saved—may be spread more widely, the infidels having been expelled, the right hand of God assisting.","Superna illius ordinatione qui caelestia et terrestria omnipotentis providentia moderatur sanctae suae universali ecclesiae licet immeriti praesidentes inter multiplices curas quibus assidue angimur doctoris gentium salubri sanctissima que doctrina moniti ad ea maxime aciem nostrae mentis convertimus per quae in ecclesia ipsa perpetua unitas et caritas inviolata perdurent grex que nobis commissus rectis tramitibus in viam salutis procedat ac christicolarum nomen et pretiosae crucis signum in quo fideles salvati sunt infidelibus expulsis dextera dei assistente latius diffundatur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p23","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis on the Reformation of Cardinals and Clergy","Now, after our predecessor Pope Julius II of happy memory, with the counsel and assent of our venerable brothers the cardinals of the holy Roman church (of whose number we then were), laudably, legitimately, and for reasonable causes, the Holy Spirit cooperating, had indicted the sacred general Lateran council and held five sessions in it, and had indicted the sixth session to be made on the 3rd of the Ides of the present month [March 13], we, with the said predecessor having been taken away from the midst, from certain causes then expressed and other causes moving our mind and that of the said brothers, by the counsel and assent of the same brothers prorogued the sixth session of this kind until this present day.","Sane postquam felicis recordationis Iulius papa ii praedecessor noster sacrum generale Lateranense concilium de venerabilium fratrum nostrorum sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalium de quorum numero tunc eramus consilio et assensu laudabiliter legitime et ex rationabilibus causis spiritu sancto cooperante et in eo quinque sessiones tenuerat sextam que sessionem tertio idus praesentis mensis faciendam indixerat nos dicto praedecessore de medio sublato extunc expressis et aliis causis nostrum et dictorum fratrum animum moventibus sextam sessionem huiusmodi de eorumdem fratrum consilio et assensu usque in hodiernum prorogavimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p24","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis on the Reformation of Cardinals and Clergy","Since, however, when we were constituted in lesser orders it was always implanted in our heart to see a general council celebrated as the principal cultivation of the Lord's field, now, having been assumed to the apex of the apostleship, perceiving that the due from the office of pastoral care enjoined upon us has been added to our honest and useful desire, we have undertaken this matter with more ardent vow and all alacrity of mind.","Cum autem in minoribus constitutis semper nobis insitum cordi fuerit generale concilium utpote agri dominici culturam praecipuam celebrari videndi nunc ad apostolatus apicem assumpti honesto utili que desiderio nostro debitum ex iniuncto nobis curae pastoralis officio accessisse conspicientes rem hanc ardentiori voto et tota animi alacritate suscepimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p25","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis on the Reformation of Cardinals and Clergy","Therefore, with the same sacred Lateran council approving, we approve the prorogation of this kind made by us, and we decree to prosecute the council itself until the perfection of the causes on account of which it was indicted, and especially so that, the savage wars among Christian princes and kings having been pacified and hostile arms laid down, universal and firm peace may be able to be established—for which we intend to apply all our efforts with indefatigable solicitude, leaving nothing untouched for such a salutary good—and to terminate it without delay; and we declare it to be and to have been our unchangeable mind and intention that, after the things which pertain to the praise of God and the exaltation of the aforesaid church and the concord of Christ's faithful have been completed, the holy and necessary expedition against the enemies of the catholic faith be made and, with the Most High favoring, triumph be able to be obtained from them.","Itaque prorogationem huiusmodi per nos factam approbante eodem sacro Lateranensi concilio approbamus ac concilium ipsum usque ad perfectionem causarum propter quas indictum extitit et praesertim ut inter principes et potentatus christianos bellis atrocibus pacatis hostilibus que armis depositis universalis et firma pax componi valeat pro qua indefessa solicitudine nihil pro tam salubri bono intactum relinquendo omnes conatus nostros adhibere intendimus prosequi et sine debito terminare velle nostri que incommutabilis animi et intentionis fore et esse declaramus ut iis quae ad dei laudem ecclesiae que praefatae exaltationem ac christi fidelium concordiam pertinent peractis sancta ac necessaria expeditio contra catholicae fidei hostes fieri feliciter que ex illis altissimo favente triumphari possit."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p26","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis on the Reformation of Cardinals and Clergy","So that those who ought to come to so useful a council may not be in any way drawn back from such access, to all and singular convoked to the celebration of the aforesaid council by the said predecessor Julius, or who ought by law or custom to be present at the congregations of general councils—especially of the Gallic nation—coming to the said Lateran council, except only schismatics and any others prohibited from coming by common or special law by sentence or apostolic letters of our predecessors or the apostolic see, and to the servants and familiars of those coming, of whatever state, grade, condition, or nobility they may be, ecclesiastical and secular, for themselves and for any of their goods, notwithstanding any additions of ecclesiastical and secular censures and penalties promulgated against them for any causes by law or by the aforesaid see under any forms of words and clauses generally, and which they have generally incurred—through the cities, lands, and places, terrestrial and maritime, subject to the aforesaid Roman church, for coming to the Lateran council to the city of Rome, for staying in it, for freely consulting, and for departing from it as often as it shall seem expedient, we give and grant by the series of these presents free, safe, full, and every kind of safe-conduct with full, free, safe, and every kind of security, and in true and infallible pontifical faith, the aforesaid sacred council approving.","Ut autem ad tantum perutile concilium accedere debentes ab accessu huiusmodi quomodolibet non retrahantur omnibus et singulis ad celebrationem concilii praefati per dictum Iulium praedecessorem convocatis aut in congregationibus conciliorum generalium de iure vel consuetudine interesse debentibus praesertim Gallicae nationis et ad dictum concilium Lateranense venientibus schismaticis et aliis quibuscumque de iure communi vel speciali per sententiam seu literas apostolicas praedecessorum nostrorum vel sedis apostolicae prohibitis dumtaxat exceptis venientium que servitoribus et familiaribus cuiuscumque status gradus conditionis vel nobilitatis existentibus ecclesiasticis et saecularibus pro se et eorum bonis quibuscumque non obstantibus quibusvis censurarum et poenarum ecclesiasticarum et saecularium adiectionibus contra eos ex quibusvis causis a iure vel per sedem praefatam sub quibusvis verborum formis et clausulis generaliter promulgatis et quas generaliter incurrerunt per civitates terras et loca terrestria et maritima Romanae ecclesiae praefatae subiecta ad concilium Lateranense ad Urbem huiusmodi veniendi et in ea morandi et libere consulendi et ab ea quoties videbitur recedendi liberum tutum plenum et omnimodum salvumconductum cum plena libera tuta et omnimoda securitate atque in vera et infallibili fide pontificia approbante sacro concilio praefato harum serie damus et concedimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p27","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis on the Reformation of Cardinals and Clergy","And nonetheless, we shall exhort, admonish, and request by our letters all and singular kings, princes, and Christian potentates that for the reverence of the Most High God and the apostolic see they not molest those coming to this sacred Lateran council directly or indirectly in any way in person or goods, nor cause them to be molested, but permit them to come freely, securely, and quietly.","Et nihilo minus omnes et singulos reges principes et potentatus christianos per literas nostras hortabimur monebimus et requiremus ut pro summi dei et apostolicae sedis reverentia venientes ad sacrum Lateranense concilium huiusmodi directe vel indirecte quomodolibet in persona vel bonis non molestent seu molestari faciant sed eos libere secure et quiete venire permittant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p28","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis on the Reformation of Cardinals and Clergy","Moreover, for the purpose of executing the celebration of the council itself, we indict the seventh session to be made on the 10th of the Kalends of June [May 23] next to come.","Ceterum pro exequenda celebratione ipsius concilii septimam sessionem decimo kalendas iunii proxime futuri faciendam indicimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p29","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis on the Reformation of Cardinals and Clergy","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p30","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis (continued)","Leo, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Leo episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p31","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis (continued)","The solicitude of apostolic governance continually presses us to apply solicitous care with the healing medicine of oil and wine, after the manner of the Samaritan in the gospel, to healing the languors of souls—the care of which the omnipotent Author from on high willed us to have—especially to those things which are now seen more urgently to press upon the faithful, lest that of Jeremiah be objected to us: \"Is there no balm in Gilead? Or is there no physician there?\"","Apostolici regiminis sollicitudo nos assidue pulsat ut medendis animarum languoribus quarum nos ex alto omnipotens auctor curam habere voluit iis potissimum qui instantius fideles nunc urgere cernuntur salutifero olei et vini medicamine ad instar samaritani in evangelio solicitam operam impendamus ne nobis illud ieremiae obiiciatur: numquid resina non est in Galaad aut medicus non est ibi."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p32","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis (continued)","Since, therefore, in our days—which we sorrowfully bear—the ancient enemy of the human race, the sower of zizania, has dared to oversow and increase in the Lord's field certain most pernicious errors always exploded by the faithful, especially concerning the nature of the rational soul—namely, that it is mortal or one in all men—and some rashly philosophizing assert according to philosophy at least that this is true—wishing to apply opportune remedies against such pestilence, with this sacred council approving, we condemn and reprobate all asserting the intellective soul to be mortal or one in all men, and those turning these things into doubt, since that [soul] not only truly, by itself and essentially, is the form of the human body, just as is contained in the canon of our predecessor Pope Clement V of happy memory issued in the general Council of Vienne, but also is immortal, and for the multitude of bodies into which it is infused, is individually multiplied, multiplied, and to be multiplied.","Cum itaque diebus nostris quod dolenter ferimus zizaniae seminator antiquus humani generis hostis nonnullos perniciosissimos errores a fidelibus semper explosos in agro domini superseminare et augere sit ausus de natura praesertim animae rationalis quod videlicet mortalis sit aut unica in cunctis hominibus et nonnulli temere philosophantes secundum saltem philosophiam verum id esse asseverant contra huiusmodi pestem opportuna remedia adhibere cupientes hoc sacro approbante concilio damnamus et reprobamus omnes asserentes animam intellectivam mortalem esse aut unicam in cunctis hominibus et haec in dubium vertentes cum illa non solum vere per se et essentialiter humani corporis forma existat sicut in canone felicis recordationis Clementis papae v praedecessoris nostri in generali Viennensi concilio edito continetur verum et immortalis et pro corporum quibus infunditur multitudine singulariter multiplicabilis et multiplicata et multiplicanda sit."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p33","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis (continued)","Which is manifestly clear from the gospel, since the Lord says: \"They cannot kill the soul\"; and elsewhere: \"He who hates his soul in this world keeps it for eternal life\"; and since He promises eternal rewards and eternal punishments to those to be judged according to the merit of life, otherwise the incarnation and other mysteries of Christ would have profited us little, nor would the resurrection be to be hoped for, and the saints and just would be, according to the apostle, more miserable than all men.","Quod manifeste constat ex evangelio cum dominus ait: animam autem occidere non possunt. Et alibi: qui odit animam suam in hoc mundo in vitam aeternam custodit eam. Et cum aeterna praemia et aeterna supplicia pro merito vitae iudicandis repromittit alias incarnatio et alia christi mysteria nobis minime profuissent nec resurrectio expectanda foret ac sancti et iusti miserabiliores essent iuxta apostolum cunctis hominibus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p34","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis (continued)","And since the true does not at all contradict the true, we define that every assertion contrary to the truth of illuminated faith is altogether false, and we more strictly inhibit that it be lawful to dogmatize otherwise; and we decree that all adhering to the assertions of such error, as sowing most damnable heresies, are to be avoided and punished as detestable and abominable heretics and infidels undermining the catholic faith in all respects.","Cum que verum vero minime contradicat omnem assertionem veritati illuminatae fidei contrariam omnino falsam esse definimus et ut aliter dogmatizare non liceat districtius inhibemus omnes que huiusmodi erroris assertionibus inhaerentes veluti damnatissimas haereses seminantes per omnia ut detestabiles et abhominabiles haereticos et infideles catholicam fidem labefactantes vitandos et puniendos fore decernimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p35","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis (continued)","Moreover, to all and singular philosophers publicly lecturing in the universities of general studies and elsewhere, we strictly command by enjoining that when they read or explain to their hearers the principles or conclusions of philosophers in which they are known to deviate from right faith—such as this concerning the mortality or unity of the soul and the eternity of the world and other such things—they be held to make the truth of the Christian religion manifest to them with all effort, and to teach it by persuading as far as possible, and to exclude and resolve with all study the arguments of such philosophers—since all [such arguments] are soluble—according to their strength.","Insuper omnibus et singulis philosophis in universitatibus studiorum generalium et alibi publice legentibus districte praecipiendo mandamus ut cum philosophorum principia aut conclusiones in quibus a recta fide deviare noscuntur auditoribus suis legerint seu explanaverint quale hoc de animae mortalitate aut unitate et mundi aeternitate ac alia huiusmodi teneantur eisdem veritatem religionis christianae omni conatu manifestam facere et persuadendo pro posse docere ac omni studio huiusmodi philosophorum argumenta cum omnia solubilia existant pro viribus excludere atque resolvere."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p36","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis (continued)","And since it sometimes does not suffice to cut off the roots of thorns unless they be utterly pulled out lest they sprout again, and to remove their seeds and original causes from which they easily arise—especially since the studies of human philosophy, longer than God—according to the word of the apostle—has made them vain and foolish, without the seasoning of divine wisdom, and which without the light of revealed truth sometimes more lead into error than into the elucidation of truth—to remove all occasion of erring in the premises, by this healthful constitution we ordain and decree that no one henceforth constituted in sacred orders, secular or regular, or otherwise bound to them by law, in general studies or elsewhere, in publicly hearing the studies of philosophy or poetry, after grammar and dialectic, remain in them beyond five years without some study of theology or pontifical law.","Et cum non sufficiat aliquando tribulorum radices praescindere nisi et ne iterum pullulent funditus evellere ac eorum semina originales que causas unde facile oriuntur removere cum praecipue humanae philosophiae studia diuturniora quam deus secundum verbum apostoli evacuavit et stultam fecit absque divinae sapientiae condimento et quae sine revelatae veritatis lumine in errorem quandoque magis inducunt quam in veritatis elucidationem ad tollendam omnem in praemissis errandi occasionem hac salutari constitutione ordinamus et statuimus ne quisquam de cetero in sacris ordinibus constitutus saecularis vel regularis aut alias ad illos a iure arctatus in studiis generalibus vel alibi publice audiendo philosophiae aut poesis studiis ultra quinquennium post grammaticam et dialecticam sine aliquo studio theologiae aut iuris pontificii incumbat."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p37","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis (continued)","But the said five years having been completed, if he wishes to sweat from those studies, it shall be free for him—provided that he simultaneously or separately give effort either to theology or to the sacred canons—so that the priests of the Lord may find in these holy and useful professions wherewith they may be able to purge and heal the infected roots of philosophy and poetry.","Verum dicto exacto quinquennio si ex illis studiis insudare voluerit liberum sit ei dum tamen simul aut seorsum aut theologiae aut sacris canonibus operam navaverit ut in his sanctis et utilibus professionibus sacerdotes domini inveniant unde infectas philosophiae et poesis radices purgare et sanare valeant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p38","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis (continued)","And we command these canons to be published by the ordinaries of the places where general studies flourish and by the rectors of the universities of the same studies, each year at the beginning of the study, in virtue of holy obedience.","Et hos canones per ordinarios locorum ubi generalia studia vigent et rectores universitatis eorumdem studiorum singulis annis in principio studii in virtute sanctae obedientiae publicari mandamus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p39","Constitution Supernae Dispositionis (continued)","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p40","Session VI (may 23, 1513)","Leo, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for future remembrance of the matter.","Leo episcopus servus servorum dei ad futuram rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p41","Session VI (may 23, 1513)","To the praise and glory of the omnipotent and individual Trinity, of Him whose vicar we bear on earth and who nourishes peace and concord in the heights, and as he departed from the world left that peace itself to his disciples by a certain hereditary right—prosecuting the sacred Lateran council, since otherwise among other things in the seventh next preceding session, and because of the imminent and notorious peril of the infidels and the effusion of Christian blood which even then was being poured out on account of our sins, it was provided, and dissensions among Christian kings and princes and peoples were to be removed, and we were compelled to seek peace with all our strength among them—for the composing of which peace was one of the principal causes of the congregation of the aforesaid council, so that that peace itself might follow and endure inviolate and be demanded for due execution, especially in these times in which the power of the infidels was known to have grown wonderfully—with the same sacred council approving, we resolved and decreed to send to the aforesaid kings, princes, and potentates legates and nuncios, watchmen of peace, distinguished in doctrine, experience, and probity, for treating and composing universal peace, and to labor and work according to our strength, the Lord helping, as much as was in us; and we requested the orators of the kings, princes, and potentates aforesaid then present in the council itself, for the reverence of the apostolic see and the union of the faithful, that they signify these things to their kings, princes, and potentates, and exhort them in our name to treat and hear the apostolic legates benignly and honorably, and to satisfy our vows—so just and pious—to be set forth to them by the same [legates].","Ad omnipotentis et individuae trinitatis laudem et gloriam illius cuius vicem in terris gerimus et qui pacem et concordiam nutrit in sublimibus pacem que ipsam e mundo discedens iure quodam haereditario suis reliquit discipulis sacrum Lateranense concilium prosequentes cum alias inter cetera in septima proxima praecedenti sessione et imminenti et notorio infidelium periculo christiani que sanguinis effusioni qui exigentibus nostris culpis etiam tunc effundebatur occurreretur dissensiones que inter christianos reges et principes et gentes tollerentur et inter ipsos totis viribus pacem quaerere cogeremur cuius componendae una ex principalioribus congregationibus concilii praefati causa extiterat ut pax ipsa sequeretur inviolata que perduraret et debitae executioni demandaretur hiis praesertim temporibus quibus potentiam infidelium mirum in modum crevisse cognoscebatur eodem sacro concilio approbante ad reges principes potentatus que praefatos legatos et nuntios pacis vigiles doctrina experientia ac probitate insignes pro tractanda et componenda universali pace mittere disposuimus et decrevimus ut que arma deponant domino auxiliante quantum in nobis esset pro viribus laborare et operari regum que principum et potentatuum praedictorum oratores in ipso concilio tunc praesentes pro apostolicae sedis reverentia fidelium que unione requisivimus ut regibus principibus et potentatibus suis ea significarent eos que nostro nomine hortarentur ad ipsos legatos apostolicos benigne et honorifice tractandum et audiendum votis que nostris tam iustis et piis per eos illis exponendis satisfaciendum."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p42","Session VI (may 23, 1513)","We persuaded ourselves that they would do this, so that, as quickly as possible, they might be able to undertake with alacrity the office of legation imposed on them by us, and having undertaken it, to fulfill it manfully, and the Father of lights, from whom is every best gift, favoring, that peace itself might be treated and composed, and that, peace having been composed, the holy and necessary expedition against the rage of the infidels panting to be satiated with Christian blood might be able to be made, and to be happily terminated for the security and quiet of the whole of Christendom.","Quod eos facturos nobis persuadebamus ut quanto citius fieri posset per nos mittendi legationis munus eis impositum alacriter suscipere susceptum que viriliter implere possent ac patre luminum a quo omne datum optimum est favente pax ipsa tractari atque componi ea que composita sancta et necessaria contra infidelium rabiem christiano sanguine satiari anhelantem expeditio fieri valeret et pro totius christianitatis securitate et quiete feliciter terminari."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p43","Session VI (may 23, 1513)","And we afterwards, for the duty of the pastoral office, wishing that peace and unity itself with the whole Christian people, but especially with the same kings, princes, and potentates from whose dissension it was feared that longer and graver injuries might day by day arise to the Christian republic—from whose authority it was hoped that the peace and unity of the same republic would be usefully and healthfully provided—sent our nuncios and letters to the aforesaid kings, princes, and potentates dissenting among themselves, to exhort, request, and admonish them, nor did we omit anything as far as was in us to treat and work with all our effort, so that, all dissensions and discords having been removed, they might wish with unanimous consent, grace, and love to return to universal peace, concord, and union, lest further injuries be inflicted on Christians by the most savage tyrant of the Turks and other infidels, but that they might gird themselves to repress the savage fury and raised attempts of those peoples.","Nos que postmodum pro debito pastoralis officii pacem et unitatem ipsam cum universo populo christiano tum maxime eisdem regibus principibus et potentatibus ex intimo cordis affectu optantes ex quorum dissensione diuturniora et graviora in dies christianae reipublicae damna provenire posse formidabatur ex eorum auctoritate pace et unitate eidem reipublicae utiliter et salubriter consultum iri sperabatur nuntios nostros et literas ad praefatos reges principes et potentatus inter se dissidentes hortandos requirendos monendos que misimus nihil que quantum in nobis fuit omisimus tractare ac operari omni nostro conatu ut semotis quibusvis dissidiis atque discordiis unanimi tandem consensu gratia et amore ad universalem pacem concordiam et unionem redire vellent ne ulterius per immanissimum Turcarum tyrannum ac infideles alios damna christianis inferrentur sed ad reprimendum gentium illarum immanem furorem elatos que conatus accingerentur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p44","Session VI (may 23, 1513)","Which things being so, we, desiring with all thought, care, study, and sedulousness that all things be brought to the desired end, and trusting, the Lord giving, that legates from our side—cardinals of the holy Roman church to be named by us afterwards in our secret consistory by the counsel of our brothers, with the necessary and opportune authority and faculties as angels of peace—for treating, composing, and conducting such universal peace of Christians and for undertaking the expedition against the infidels, with the same sacred council approving, should be sent and destined to the same kings, for inducing them with prompt and alacritous spirit to each most holy work of peace and expedition, according to their royal magnanimity and devotion toward the catholic faith, for the entire and perfect protection, defense, and salvation of the whole Christian republic, we decree.","Quae cum ita sint nos cuncta ad optatum finem perduci omni cogitatione cura studio ac sedulitate affectantes et dante domino confidentes legatos de latere nostro sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinales in consistorio nostro secreto de fratrum nostrorum consilio per nos postmodum nominandos cum auctoritate et facultatibus necessariis et opportunis tanquam angelos pacis pro huiusmodi christianorum pace universali tractanda componenda et conducenda expeditione que contra infideles ineunda eodem sacro concilio approbante ad eosdem reges pro eorum regia magnanimitate et erga catholicam fidem devotione prompto et alacri animo ad utrumque sanctissimum opus pacis expeditionis que inducendos pro universae reipublicae christianae integra atque perfecta tutela defensione et salute mittendos et destinandos esse decernimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p45","Session VI (may 23, 1513)","Moreover, since from the long and manifold heresy of the Bohemians God is greatly offended and the Christian people scandalized—the province of reducing them also to the light of true faith and concord having been fully entrusted by us in these recent days to our beloved son Thomas, priest cardinal of the title of Saint Martin in the Mountains, archbishop of Strigonia, our legate to Hungary and Bohemia—we nevertheless exhort them in the Lord that they not neglect to send some of their orators with sufficient mandate either to us and this sacred Lateran council or to the same Thomas cardinal legate, who is nearer to them, to treat concerning the opportune remedy by which they may recognize the errors by which they have long been held and, God leading, be reduced to the true cult of religion and the bosom of holy mother church.","Praeterea cum ex diuturna et multiplici haeresi Bohemorum plurimum deus offendatur et christianus populus scandalizetur quorum etiam ad verae fidei lumen atque concordiam reducendorum provincia dilecto filio nostro Thomae tituli sancti Martini in Montibus presbytero cardinali Strigoniensi ad Hungariam et Bohemiam nostro et apostolicae sedis legato est a nobis his proximis diebus plene demandata eosdem tamen hortamur in domino ut vel ad nos et hoc sacrum Lateranense concilium vel ad eumdem Thomam cardinalem legatum qui propinquior illis erit aliquot ex suis oratores cum sufficienti mandato destinare non negligant ad tractandum de opportuno remedio quo errores quibus diu detinentur agnoscant et ad verum religionis cultum et sanctae matris ecclesiae gremium deo duce reducantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p46","Session VI (may 23, 1513)","To whom, with this sacred council approving, we grant and bestow by the tenor of the presents free safe-conduct and public faith in pontifical faith for coming, going, standing as long as the premises to be treated shall last, and afterwards departing and returning to their homes; and we shall assent to their vows as much as with God we can.","Quibus hoc sacro approbante concilio veniendi eundi standi quousque praemissa tractanda duraverint et postmodum discedendi et ad propria redeundi in fide pontificia liberum salvumconductum et fidem publicam tenore praesentium concedimus et elargimur eorum que votis quantum cum deo poterimus annuemus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p47","Session VI (may 23, 1513)","So that this sacred Lateran council may be brought to the fruit of most abundant hoped-for utility—for many other and grave things remain to be treated and discussed in it—for the praise of omnipotent God and the exaltation of His church, with the sacred council approving, we indict the ninth session of the continuation of the celebration of this sacred Lateran council on the 9th of April of the year 1514, the first year of our pontificate, which will be the fourth feria after the Sunday of the Passion.","Ut autem hoc sacrum Lateranense concilium ad finem uberrimae speratae que utilitatis perducatur alia namque plura et gravia in eo tractanda discutienda que remaneant ad omnipotentis dei laudem et ecclesiae suae exaltationem nonam sessionem continuationis celebrationis ipsius sacri concilii Lateranensis nono aprilis millesimo quingentesimo quartodecimo pontificatus nostri anno primo quae erit quarta feria post dominicam passionis sacro concilio approbante indicimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p48","Session VI (may 23, 1513)","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p49","Session VIII (december 19, 1513)","Leo, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Leo episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p50","Session VIII (december 19, 1513)","Placed in the watchtower of the apostolic summit by the gift of divine grace, we have judged nothing to be more of our duty than sedulously and diligently to look around at all things which could pertain to the custody, safety, and increase of the catholic flock committed to us, and to have applied to this all the sinews of industry, all the forces of mind and intellect.","In apostolici culminis specula divinae gratiae munere collocati nihil magis esse nostri officii duximus quam sedulo ac diligenter omnia circumspicere quae ad catholici nobis commissi gregis custodiam incolumitatem augmentum pertinere possent et in hoc ipsum omnes industriae nervos omnes animi ingenii que vires contulimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p51","Session VIII (december 19, 1513)","Since, therefore, our predecessor Pope Julius II of happy memory, as he was solicitous for the convenience of the faithful and studious for their protection, both for many other causes and because frequent complaint about the officials of the Roman curia was pressing, had indicted the ecumenical Lateran council, and, various congregations of his venerable brothers the cardinals of the holy Roman church (of whose number we then were) and also of prelates having been deputed who would accurately inquire into the complaints themselves, and lest in the meantime the curiales and others coming to the curia for graces be fatigued by too great a burden of expenses, and at the same time so that the infamy with which the said officials were branded might be mitigated, had issued a bull of reformation by which they were again bound under grave penalty to observe the statutes of the offices, and, prevented by death, had not been able to decree specially concerning the excesses or to absolve the council—we, his successors in care no less than in office, at the beginning of our pontificate, both to continue the synod and to procure peace among Christian princes and no less to complete universal reformation, have not ceased to support with new aids what had been provided by the predecessor himself concerning the offices, and to execute it with increased deputations.","Cum igitur felicis recordationis Iulius papa ii praedecessor noster ut erat fidelium commodi solicitus et tutelae studiosus tum aliis multis de causis tum quia frequens circa Romanae curiae officiales querimonia urgebat oecumenicum Lateranense concilium indixisset et deputatis variis venerabilium fratrum suorum sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalium de quorum numero tunc eramus et praelatorum insuper congregationibus qui de querelis ipsis accurate inquirerent et ne curiales interim et alii ad curiam pro gratiis accedentes nimio expensarum onere fatigarentur simul ut infamia qua dicti officiales flagrabant mitigaretur celeri remedio bullam reformationis edidisset qua illi officiorum statutis custodiendis sub gravi poena denuo astringebantur nec circa excessus specialiter decernere aut concilium absolvere morte praeventus quisivisset Nos eius non minus curae quam officii successores initio statim nostri pontificatus et continuare synodum et pacem inter christianos principes procurare nec minus cum universalem reformationem perficere nostrae mentis sit primum a praedecessore ipso super officiis provisa novis fulcire subsidiis et auctis deputationibus exequi non destitimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p52","Session VIII (december 19, 1513)","For indeed no more vehement care gnaws at us than that thorns and briers be rooted out of the Lord's field, and if there be anything that hinders its cultivation, it be torn up and extirpated from the roots.","Enimvero nulla vehementior cura nos angit quam ut tribuli et sentes ex dominico agro eruantur et si quid est quod eius culturae officiat radicitus evellatur atque extirpetur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p53","Session VIII (december 19, 1513)","Therefore, a sedulous report having been had from the deputed persons, and having observed what had deviated from what by whom, each thing which had departed from decent and laudable custom or from primeval institution, we reduced to the norm, and we gathered them in order into one bull of reformation issued concerning this with the sacred council approving; in it we deputed executors who would compel the ordained things to be observed; and we command, with this sacred council approving, that it be observed without fraud and inviolably by the officials themselves and others as touches each one, under penalty of the sentence of excommunication latae sententiae, from which they cannot be absolved except by the Roman pontiff—except in the article of death—so that besides this and other penalties particularly expressed in that bull, those contravening are ipso facto suspended for a six-month period from the office in which they have offended.","Sedula ergo a deputatis relatione habita et quid a quibus exorbitaretur animadverso ut quaeque vel a decenti et laudabili more vel a primaeva institutione desciverant ad normam retraximus et ea in unam reformationis bullam sacro approbante concilio super hoc editam ex ordine congessimus in ea que executores qui ordinata servari cogerent deputavimus quam ab officialibus ipsis et ceteris ut quemque tanget sine fraude et inviolabiliter observari hoc sacro approbante concilio mandamus sub poena excommunicationis latae sententiae a qua nisi per Romanum pontificem excepto mortis articulo absolvi non possint ita ut ultra hanc et alias poenas in bulla ipsa particulariter expressas contravenientes officio in quo deliquerint ad semestre tempus sint eo ipso suspensi."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p54","Session VIII (december 19, 1513)","And if they shall have erred a second time in the same office, let them be perpetually deprived of that which, as far as in them lay, they have defiled.","Et si secundo erraverint in eodem officio quod quantum in se fuit coinquinarunt perpetuo careant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p55","Session VIII (december 19, 1513)","After they shall have been reduced to good fruit by our constitution, the common injury having been inhibited and removed, we shall proceed to the other parts of reformation.","Qui postquam constitutione nostra ad frugem reducti erunt inhibito atque sublato communi damno ad ceteras reformationis partes procedemus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p56","Session VIII (december 19, 1513)","And if omnipotent God, for His mercy, shall grant to us to compose peace among Christian princes, we shall insist not only on extirpating noxious seeds but also on spreading the boundaries of Christ, and, God Himself favoring His own cause, supported by the aid of the same, we shall undertake the most holy expedition against the infidels, the desire of which continually inheres in our heart.","Et si nobis omnipotens pro sua misericordia concesserit ut pacem inter christianos principes componamus non noxiis solum seminibus extirpandis sed et propagandis christi finibus insistemus ac sanctissimam contra infideles expeditionem cuius desiderium assidue nostro cordi inhaeret deo ipso rei suae favente eorumdem fulti praesidiis aggrediemur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p57","Session VIII (december 19, 1513)","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p58","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","Leo, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Leo episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p59","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","After we were called by divine dispensation to the care and governance of the universal church, although unequal to so great a burden, from the highest summit of the apostleship, as from the peak of Mount Zion, we began to look out and to revolve in mind those things which seemed primarily to pertain to the salvation, tranquillity, and propagation of the church itself.","Postquam ad universalis ecclesiae curam et regimen licet tanto oneri impares divina dispensatione vocati fuimus ex summo statim apostolatus apice tamquam ex vertice montis Sion ea prospicere ac animo volvere coepimus quae ad salutem tranquillitatem ac propagationem ipsius ecclesiae pertinere in primis videbantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p60","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","And when we turned all our care, thought, and study to this matter, after the manner of a skilled and watchful shepherd, we found nothing more grievous, more pernicious to the Christian republic, or more adverse to our pious desire than the savage rage of wars, which has so much advanced in arms that Italy, almost laid waste by mutual losses, cities having been deformed and partly overthrown, partly levelled, lands shaken, provinces and kingdoms do not cease to rage and rave against Christian blood.","Cum que in hanc rem omnem nostram curam cogitationem ac studium more solertis vigilis que pastoris converteremus nihil gravius aut christianae reipublicae perniciosius et pio nostro desiderio magis adversum bellorum effera rabie comperimus quae usque adeo armis grassata est ut vastata pene mutuis cladibus Italia deformatis que ac partim eversis aequatis partim solo civitatibus et terris concussis provinciis et regnis in christianum sanguinem furere ac debacchari non desinant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p61","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","Therefore we judged nothing to be done sooner, nothing more studiously or more from the heart, than to calm those wars, to compose ecclesiastical discipline according to our strength and the condition of times, so that, God having been placated through the amendment of life, the Lord's flock, entrusted to our faith, the dissensions having been composed, we might be able to gather and assemble it into one, and, united by the bond of peace and concord as by a very strong glue, more easily to exhort and incite it against the common enemies of the faith hanging over the necks of Christians.","Nihil itaque prius nihil studiosius aut magis ex animo curandum duximus quam ea bella sedare ecclesiasticam que disciplinam pro viribus ac temporum ratione componere ut placato per vitae emendationem deo dominicum gregem nostrae fidei commissum compositis dissidiis in unum cogere et aggregare unanimem que ac pacis et concordiae vinculo tamquam firmissimo glutino unitum adversus communes hostes fidei christianis cervicibus imminentes facilius adhortari ac concitare possimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p62","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","Of which expedition against the impious and implacable enemies of the cross of Christ, this vehement desire of ours is so fixed in our heart that we decreed to continue and prosecute the sacred Lateran council, indicted and begun by our predecessor Julius II of happy memory and interrupted by his death, for this cause especially, as is manifest to all in the several sessions held by us in the same sacred council, so that, peace among Christian kings and princes having been composed—their orators assembled in that council and prelates coming to it from various parts of the world—the thorns of pestiferous heresies having been extirpated first, as is fitting, from the Lord's field, those things which pertain to the expedition itself against the same enemies and to the glory and exaltation of the orthodox faith and the consultation of other matters might be able happily to be decreed with mature counsel and the assent of all.","Cuius quidem expeditionis in impios et crucis christi implacabiles inimicos hoc vehemens nostrum desiderium ita cordi nostro infixum est ut sacrum Lateranense concilium a felicis recordationis Iulio ii praedecessore nostro indictum ac inchoatum eius que obitu intermissum ea praecipue causa continuare et prosequi decrevimus ut in diversis sessionibus in eodem sacro concilio per nos habitis omnibus patet ut christianis principibus seu eorum oratoribus in eo concilio congregatis praelatis que ex diversis mundi partibus eo venientibus pace inter ipsos christianos principes composita extirpatis que ut par est primo ex domini agro pestiferis haeresum sentibus ea quae ad ipsam expeditionem contra eosdem hostes necessaria ac ad gloriam et exaltationem orthodoxae fidei aliarum ve rerum consultationem pertinent maturo consilio omnium que assensu decerni feliciter possent."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p63","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","To which council, although solemnly indicted and duly announced, many distinguished men excelling in all knowledge from various parts of Europe have come, many also legitimately impeded have sent mandates in public form, nevertheless, on account of the difficulties of wars and times, by which very many places have long been besieged by hostile arms, the abundance and multitude which we desired could not convene.","Ad quod quidem concilium solemniter indictum rite que denuntiatum etsi multi ex diversis Europae partibus praeclari et in omni scientia praestantes viri convenerint multi etiam legitime impediti mandata in publica forma miserint non tamen bellorum ac temporum difficultatibus quibus loca plurima iam diu hostilibus armis obsessa fuerunt ea quam desiderabamus copia et multitudo convenire potuit."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p64","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","There is nothing, moreover, that can be ascribed to us if we have not sent legates from our side to kings and princes for the concord of the same kings and peace—which perhaps seems necessary to many and we think opportune above others. For we have desisted from doing this for this cause: for almost all the princes have signified by their letters and nuncios to us that it is by no means expedient or necessary that legates be sent.","Nihil est praeterea quod nobis adscribi possit si hactenus quod forsitan multis necessarium videtur et nos opportunum prae ceteris putamus legatos de latere ad reges et principes pro concordia eorumdem principum et pace non misimus. Hac sane causa id facere destitimus. Principes enim fere omnes suis ad nos literis ac nuntiis legatos mitti minime expedire aut necessarium esse significarunt."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p65","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","Nevertheless, we have of our own accord sent to those same kings—waging more grievous wars among themselves and, as far as could be guessed, harsher battles—circumspect men and of proved faith, our nuncios, also endowed with episcopal dignity.","Circumspectos nihilo minus ac probatae fidei viros nuntios nostros etiam episcopali dignitate praeditos ad eos ipsos principes gravior inter se armis ac quantum coniici poterat asperiora bella molientes ultro misimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p66","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","By whose work especially it has been brought about that, truces having already been agreed upon among certain princes, the others are believed to be about to approve them shortly. Wherefore, we shall not postpone to send legates, as we decreed in the next session, where it shall be opportune and expedient for composing stable and perpetual peace among them, and as we proposed earlier.","Quorum praecipue opera effectum est ut pactis iam inter quosdam principes induciis ceteri eas brevi approbaturi esse credantur. Quare legatos uti proxima sessione decrevimus ubi opus et expediens fuerit pro stabili ac perpetua pace inter eos componenda ac uti antea proposuimus mittere non postponemus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p67","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","Nor in the meantime shall we cease to treat and meditate with the orators of the same kings acting with us concerning things which pertain to this matter, and to urge and admonish them and the kings through our nuncios and letters.","Neque interim cum eorumdem principum oratoribus apud nos agentibus quae ad rem huiusmodi pertinent agere et meditari neque ipsos et principes per nostros nuntios et literas ad id urgere et admonere cessabimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p68","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","Yea, moreover, so that omnipotent and merciful God may promote from on high our counsels of peace and unceasing thoughts, and look upon the faithful people with more just and propitious eyes, and for the common salvation and peace, and for restraining the proud rage of the nefarious enemies of the Christian name, may assent to His pious prayers—by apostolic authority we enjoin and in virtue of holy obedience command all and singular primates, patriarchs, archbishops, bishops, and also the chapters, colleges, convents of churches both cathedral and collegiate, both secular and of any orders regular, and the plebans, deans, rectors of churches, and others having the care of souls, preachers, questors, and those proclaiming the word of God to the people, that within the solemnities of masses and when the word of God is set forth to the people during or outside them, and in their prayers which they shall make capitularly and conventually or otherwise in any congregations, they have these special collects for the peace of Christians and the confusion of infidels, namely: Deus a quo sancta desideria and Deus in cuius manu sunt omnes potestates et omnia iura regnorum—Respice in auxilium christianorum.","Quin etiam ut nostra pacis consilia et iuges cogitatus omnipotens et misericors deus promoveat ex alto ac fidelem plebem aequis ac propitiis magis oculis aspiciat et pro communi salute ac pace compescenda que nefariorum christiani nominis hostium superba rabie eius piis annuat precibus universis et singulis primatibus patriarchis archiepiscopis episcopis nec non ecclesiarum tam cathedralium quam collegiatarum tam que saecularium quam quorumvis ordinum regularium capitulis collegiis conventibus plebanis decanis ecclesiarum rectoribus ceteris que animarum curam habentibus praedicatoribus quaestoribus ac verbum dei ad populum pronuntiantibus auctoritate apostolica iniungimus et in virtute sanctae obedientiae mandamus quatenus intra missarum solemnia et dum inter aut extra illa verbum dei ad populum proponetur ac in eorum orationibus quas capitulariter et conventualiter aut alias in quibuscumque congregationibus facient respective pro pace christianorum et infidelium confusione has collectas habeant speciales videlicet deus a quo sancta desideria et deus in cuius manu sunt omnes potestates et omnia iura regnorum respice in auxilium christianorum."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p69","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","And nonetheless, let them enjoin upon their diocesans and upon any other persons of both sexes, both ecclesiastical and secular, over whom they preside by reason of prelature or any other ecclesiastical superiority, and let them exhort them in the Lord—and let those to whom the word of God is set forth by their own or another's authority proclaim it—that privately for the peace of Christians as premised and the extermination of infidels they pour forth pious prayers to God Himself and to His most glorious mother in the Lord's Prayer and in the Angelic Salutation.","Et nihilominus suis dioecesanis et aliis quibuscumque personis utriusque sexus tam ecclesiasticis quam saecularibus quibus ratione praelaturae et alterius cuiuscumque superioritatis ecclesiasticae praesidebunt iniungant ac in domino eos hortentur quibus dei verbum sua aut alterius auctoritate proponetur ut privatim pro pace ut praemittitur christianorum et infidelium exterminatione ad deum ipsum ac gloriosissimam eius matrem in oratione dominica et in angelica salutatione pias preces effundant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p70","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","And moreover, whoever of the aforesaid persons, existing among secular princes of whatever grade, pre-eminence, or dignity, or also their counselors, familiars, domestics, and officials, and also among the magistrates, rectors, lieutenants, or other persons of both sexes, ecclesiastical or secular, of cities, towns, or any secular universities, whom they shall have thought to have weight by authority and grace for persuading universal or particular peace among kings, potentates, and Christian peoples, and also the expedition against the infidels—let them exhort and induce them to such peace and expedition.","Et insuper quicumque ex supradictis apud principes saeculares cuiuscumque gradus praeeminentiae vel dignitatis existant aut etiam consiliarios familiares domesticos et officiarios eorum nec non apud civitatum oppidorum vel universitatum quorumcumque saecularium magistratus rectores locumtenentes aut alias utriusque sexus personas ecclesiasticas vel saeculares quas ad pacem universalem aut particularem inter principes potentatus et christianos populos nec non ad expeditionem contra infideles persuadendam auctoritate et gratia valere putaverint illos ad huiusmodi pacem et expeditionem hortentur et inducant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p71","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","Whom all, by the bowels of the mercy of our God and by the merit of the passion of His only-begotten Son Jesus Christ, we exhort in the Lord with the greatest possible affection of heart, and by the authority of the pastoral office which we exercise, we admonish, that, private rivalries having been laid aside and open enmities, they turn themselves to embracing the pursuits of peace and to decreeing the aforesaid expedition.","Quos omnes per viscera misericordiae dei nostri et per meritum passionis unigeniti eius filii Iesu christi quam maximo possumus cordis affectu in domino hortamur ac pro pastoralis officii qua fungimur auctoritate monemus ut depositis privatis simultatibus apertis que inimicitiis ad pacis studia amplectenda et ad expeditionem praedictam decernendam se convertant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p72","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","Wherefore we more strictly inhibit all and singular prelates, princes, and persons, both secular and ecclesiastical, of whatever state, grade, dignity, pre-eminence, or condition they may exist, under the intermination of divine judgment, that they presume in any way to interpose any impediment, directly or indirectly, openly or secretly, to the said peace to be treated by us or our legates or nuncios of the apostolic see and those endowed with episcopal dignity, as is preferred, for the defense of the Christian republic and the salvation of the faithful.","Propterea que omnibus et singulis praelatis principibus et personis tam saecularibus quam ecclesiasticis cuiuscumque status gradus dignitatis et praeeminentiae aut conditionis existant sub interminatione divini iudicii districtius inhibemus ne dictae paci per nos aut nostros seu sedis apostolicae legatos vel nuntios et episcopali dignitate praeditos ut praefertur pro christianae reipublicae defensione et fidelium salute tractandae aliquod impedimentum directe vel indirecte palam aut occulte interponere quoquo modo praesumant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p73","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","But those who pretend that private or public interest of their kings or their cities or republics—the care of which will pertain to them by reason of office or any public duty—concerns them in the composing of peace, let them embrace, as modestly and tranquilly as they can, the parts and studies of the peace that is being prosecuted, nonetheless as much as they can in the Lord.","Sed qui in pace conficienda privatum aut publicum suorum principum suarum ve civitatum aut rerumpublicarum quorum quarum ve ad eos ratione officii vel publici alicuius muneris cura spectabit interesse praetendant illud quoad modestius tranquillius ve poterunt prosequentis pacis nihilo minus partes et studia quantum in domino poterunt amplectantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p74","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","Yea, moreover, wishing to animate Christ's faithful with spiritual gifts for procuring the said peace and expedition against the said enemies of the Christian faith and for obtaining them from God Himself, to all truly confessed and contrite persons who for the purpose of obtaining that peace and decreeing the expedition shall pour forth prayers devoutly to God in masses, preachings, and other divine offices, or in collegial, conventual, or other public or common prayers, or shall employ diligent and sedulous effort with the princes, counselors, officials, rectors, or other persons aforesaid who shall seem to have any authority for making or treating peace and for decreeing the expedition against the enemies of the victorious cross as premised—as often as they shall have done this—we, those who shall have poured forth devout prayers privately and particularly for obtaining the premises from God, if they shall have done it seven times in a day, or if less, as often as they shall have done it, until the universal peace which is being diligently treated by us among kings and peoples currently dissenting by arms shall have been concluded and the expedition against the infidels decreed under our auspices, trusting in the mercy of omnipotent God and the authority of His blessed apostles Peter and Paul, relax one hundred days of their enjoined penances.","Quin etiam ad dictam pacem et expeditionem contra dictos christianae fidei hostes procurandam et ab ipso deo impetrandam volentes spiritualibus donis christi fideles animare omnibus vere confessis et contritis qui pro ipsa obtinenda pace ac decernenda expeditione in missis praedicationibus et aliis divinis officiis aut in collegialibus conventualibus aliis ve publicis aut communibus orationibus devote ad deum preces effundant aut apud principes consiliarios officiarios rectores vel alias personas supradictas quae ad pacem faciendam vel tractandam et expeditionem in hostes invictae crucis ut praemittitur decernendam aliquam auctoritatem habere videbuntur diligentem ac solertem operam adhibebunt quoties id fecerint. Qui vero privatim ac particulariter pro praemissis a deo consequendis devotas effuderint preces septies in die si toties id fecerint aut si minus quoties id fecerint donec pax universalis quae per nos assidue tractatur inter principes et populos impraesentiarum armis dissidentes confecta sit et expeditio contra infideles nostris auspiciis decreta de omnipotentis dei misericordia ac de beatorum eius apostolorum Petri et Pauli auctoritate confisi centum dies de iniunctis eis poenitentiis relaxamus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p75","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","We command therefore our venerable brothers the primates, patriarchs, archbishops, and bishops to whom the presents or copies of them faithfully printed under authentic seals either in the City or elsewhere shall come, that they cause them to be published through their provinces and dioceses as soon as they can, and to be demanded for due execution.","Mandantes propterea venerabilibus fratribus primatibus patriarchis archiepiscopis et episcopis ad quos praesentes seu earum exempla sub sigillis authenticis vel in Urbe aut alibi fideliter impressa pervenerint quatenus illas per suas provincias et dioeceses quanto citius poterunt faciant publicari ac debitae executioni demandari."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p76","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","Moreover, what we have proposed and desired with our whole heart—the ecclesiastical reformation meanwhile both of our curia and also of our venerable brothers the cardinals of the holy Roman church and of others staying in the City, and many other necessary things—with the sacred council approving, we have decreed, which shall be contained in our other letters to be published in this same session.","Ceterum quod proposuimus et toto corde concupivimus reformationem interim ecclesiasticam tam curiae nostrae quam etiam venerabilium fratrum nostrorum sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalium et aliorum in Urbe commorantium et alia complura necessaria sacro approbante concilio decrevimus quae aliis nostris litteris in hac eadem sessione publicandis continebuntur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p77","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","And although the same predecessor Julius had convoked to this council all who are accustomed to be present, and had given ample safe-conduct to them that they might be able to come and approach safely and securely, nevertheless because up to now, on account of the aforesaid impediments perhaps, several prelates who ought to come have not yet come—we, wishing to proceed to graver matters in the next forthcoming session, exhort them again in the Lord, and by the bowels of the same mercy we ask and admonish, as well as kings, dukes, margraves, counts, and others accustomed to come or send to general councils, who up to now have not given orators or legitimate mandates, that as soon as they can, they be willing either to come personally or to send special and suitable nuncios with legitimate mandates to this Lateran council, which is sacred and will be useful to the Christian republic.","Et licet idem Iulius praedecessor noster ad huiusmodi concilium omnes qui adesse consuevere convocaverit et ut tute ac secure venire et accedere possent eis amplum salvumconductum dederit quia tamen hactenus ob praedicta forsitan impedimenta complures praelati qui venire deberent adhuc non venerunt cupientes nos ad graviora in futura proxima sessione procedere denuo ipsos ac reges duces marchiones comites et alios ad concilia generalia venire aut mittere assuetos qui hactenus oratores vel mandata legitima non dederint in domino hortamur ac per eiusdem misericordiae viscera rogamus et monemus ut quamprimum poterunt vel ipsi personaliter accedere vel speciales et idoneos nuntios cum legitimis mandatis ad hoc sacrum et christianae reipublicae perutile futurum Lateranense concilium mittere velint."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p78","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","But to our venerable brothers the patriarchs, archbishops, bishops, abbots, prelates—especially bound under the bond of an oath to visit the thresholds of the apostles Peter and Paul at certain fixed times and to come personally to general councils when indicted, even at the time of their promotion—whose contumacy for not appearing has been often accused in the several sessions by the promoter of the said council, and it is found that it has been petitioned to proceed against them and solemnly protested concerning the incurrence of censures and penalties—notwithstanding any privileges, concessions, and indults granted, confirmed, and renewed to them and to their churches, monasteries, and benefices by the said our predecessors or by us, which, holding them as expressed, from certain knowledge and plenitude of power we quash, make void, and annul—in virtue of holy obedience we enjoin, and under penalty of excommunication and perjury and other [penalties] from law or custom and especially in the letters of indiction and publication of the said Lateran council specially indicted by the same our predecessor Julius, or other [penalties] for those not coming to a council duly indicted, we more strictly command that they come personally to the said Lateran council and be bound to stay in the City until it is concluded and absolved by our authority, unless they be hindered by some legitimate cause.","Venerabilibus autem fratribus patriarchis archiepiscopis episcopis abbatibus praelatis praesertim sub vinculo iurisiurandi ad visitandum certis statutis que temporibus limina apostolorum Petri et Pauli et ad generalia concilia indicta personaliter accedendum etiam tempore suae promotionis adstrictis quorum non comparentium contumacia in diversis sessionibus per promotorem dicti concilii saepius accusata extitit et contra eosdem petitum procedi et de incursu censurarum et poenarum protestatum solemniter reperitur non obstantibus quibuscumque privilegiis concessionibus et indultis eis eorum que ecclesiis monasteriis et beneficiis per dictos praedecessores nostros aut nos concessis confirmatis et innovatis quae habentes pro expressis ex certa scientia et plenitudine potestatis cassamus irritamus et annullamus in virtute sanctae obedientiae iniungimus ac sub excommunicationis et periurii aliis que de iure vel consuetudine et praesertim in literis indictionis et publicationis dicti concilii Lateranensis per ipsum Iulium praedecessorem nostrum specialiter indictis aut aliis non accedentibus ad concilium rite indictum poenis districtius mandamus ut ad dictum concilium Lateranense accedere personaliter et usquequo conclusum et auctoritate nostra absolutum fuerit in Urbe morari debeant nisi aliqua legitima causa impediti fuerint."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p79","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","And if, as preferred, they be hindered, let them send their special proctors, suitable for those things which are to be treated, done, and deliberated, with full mandate.","Et si ut praefertur modo fuerint impediti procuratores suos speciales cum pleno mandato ad ea quae tractanda agenda et deliberanda erunt idoneos mittant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p80","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","And so that all excuse whatsoever may be removed, nor any pretext of any impediment be left to anyone who ought to come, besides the public faith which it is clear was granted to all coming to this council in the indiction of this council, with this same council approving, we give and grant and impart by apostolic authority, by the tenor of the presents, with the aforesaid counsel and power, to all and singular accustomed to be present at the congregations of general councils and coming to this Lateran council itself, and to their domestics and familiars, of whatever state, grade, order, and condition or nobility they may exist, ecclesiastical and secular, for themselves and for any of their goods, through the cities, lands, and places both maritime and terrestrial subject to the said Roman church, for coming to the Lateran council to the City, for freely staying in it, for consulting according to their mind's judgment, and for departing from it as often as it shall seem expedient, and for four months after the conclusion and dissolution of the said council, free, safe, and secure safe-conduct and full and every kind of security; and we freely promise to give safe-conducts and securities to others wishing to have them; we shall treat and receive all and singular of them benignly and charitably.","Et ut omnis prorsus excusatio tollatur neve cuiquam venire debenti praetextus aliquis cuiuscumque impedimenti relinquatur praeter publicam fidem quam in indictione huius concilii omnibus ad id venientibus liquet esse concessam hoc eodem approbante concilio omnibus et singulis in congregationibus conciliorum generalium adesse solitis et ad ipsum Lateranense concilium venientibus eorum que domesticis familiaribus cuiuscumque status gradus ordinis et conditionis vel nobilitatis existant ecclesiasticis et saecularibus pro se et bonis suis quibuscumque per civitates terras et loca tam maritima quam terrestria dictae Romanae ecclesiae subiecta ad concilium Lateranense ad Urbem veniendi et in ea libere commorandi et pro animi sententia consulendi et ab ea quoties videbitur recedendi et post quatuor menses a dicti concilii conclusione et dissolutione liberum tutum et securum salvumconductum ac plenam et omnimodam securitatem auctoritate apostolica tenore praesentium de consilio et potestate praedictis damus et concedimus et impartimur alios que salvosconductus et securitates habere cupientibus dare libere pollicemur quos omnes et singulos benigne et caritative tractabimus et suscipiemus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p81","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","We command all and singular secular princes, however great the dignity with which they shine—even imperial, royal, reginal, ducal, or any other—and the rectors of cities and citizens governing and ruling their republic, that they grant free license and faculty and safe-conduct for coming and returning to the prelates and others coming to the said Lateran council, under the incurrence of divine majesty and our indignation and the penalties against those impeding the celebration of councils and especially of the said Lateran council contained in law or in the letters of the aforesaid indiction by our predecessor and imposed, and that they grant free and harmless transit through their dominions, lands, and places through which they must pass, with their goods and belongings and horses, all exceptions or excuses being altogether removed and ceasing.","Mandantes omnibus et singulis principibus saecularibus quantumcumque grandi dignitate etiam imperiali regali reginali ducali aut alia quacumque fulgentibus et civitatum rectoribus ac civibus eorum rempublicam gubernantibus et regentibus ut praelatis et aliis ad dictum concilium Lateranense venientibus sub divinae maiestatis et indignationis nostrae incursu et poenis contra impedientes conciliorum celebrationem et praesertim dicti Lateranensis concilii in iure sive literis prefatae indictionis per praedecessorem nostrum contentis et appositis liberam licentiam et facultatem salvum que conductum veniendi et redeundi et per eorum dominia terras et loca per quae ipsos transitum facere oporteat cum eorum rebus et bonis ac equis liberum transitum et innoxium concedant omnibus prorsus exceptionibus sive excusationibus semotis ac cessantibus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p82","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","We command and enjoin, under the penalty of our indignation and other [penalties] to be inflicted at our discretion, all and singular our peoples, armigers, both foot and horse, and their dukes and leaders, and also the castellans of our fortresses and the legates, governors, rectors, lieutenants, podestàs, officials, and vassals of the cities and places subject to the said Roman church, that, notwithstanding any apostolic constitutions, ordinances, imperial laws, statutes, and municipal customs—even fortified by oath and apostolic confirmation or any other firmness—which could derogate in any way from the aforesaid safe-conduct and security or impede them in any way, even if they were such of which special, specific, express, and individual mention, not through general clauses importing this, or any other expression would have to be had—holding the tenors of all of them as expressed as if inserted word for word into the presents—they permit and cause to be permitted those coming to the Lateran council to pass, stay, and depart freely, safely, and securely, so that so holy, laudable, and very necessary a council be not disturbed by any cause or pretext, and that those coming to it may be able to live, speak, and expedite safely and in peace, tranquillity, and liberty those things which concern the honor of omnipotent God and the state of the whole church.","Mandamus insuper et iniungimus sub indignationis nostrae et aliis arbitrio nostro infligendis poenis omnibus et singulis gentibus nostris armigeris tam pedestribus quam equestribus eorum que ducibus et ductoribus ac etiam arcium nostrarum castellanis civitatum que et locorum dictae Romanae ecclesiae subiectorum legatis gubernatoribus rectoribus locum tenentibus potestatibus officialibus et vassallis ut quibusvis constitutionibus ordinationibus apostolicis legibus imperialibus statutis que et consuetudinibus municipalibus etiam iuramento et confirmatione apostolica aut quavis alia firmitate roboratis non obstantibus quae salvoconductui et securitati praemissis in aliquo derogare seu illa quoquo modo impedire possent etiam si talia forent de quibus specialis et specifica expressa et individua non autem per generales clausulas id importantes mentio seu quaevis alia expressio habenda foret illorum omnium tenores praesentibus ac si de verbo ad verbum insererentur pro expressis habentes et aliis quibuscumque cuiuscumque gradus status conditionis et praeeminentiae fuerint ut ipsos venientes ad concilium Lateranense libere tute et secure transire morari et recedere permittant et permitti faciant ne tam sanctum laudabile ac pernecessarium concilium quavis causa vel praetextu distrahatur et ut ad illud venientes tute ac in pacis tranquillitate libertate que vivere dicere et expedire valeant quae honorem dei omnipotentis statum que totius ecclesiae concernant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p83","Session IX (may 5, 1514)","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p84","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Leo, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Leo episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p85","Session X (may 4, 1515)","By the ordinance of the supernal disposition, by which heavenly things and earthly alike are disposed by ineffable providence, it behooves the Roman pontiff, sitting on the sublime throne of blessed Peter, bearing the place of Christ the only-begotten Son of God on earth, first of all, for the care and salvation of the Lord's flock divinely committed to him, to exercise the office of a provident shepherd.","Supernae dispositionis arbitrio quo caelestia pariter et terrena ineffabili providentia disponuntur in sublimi beati Petri solio christi unigeniti dei filii gerentem vices in terris Romanum pontificem decet in primis pro cura et salute dominici gregis sibi divinitus commissi providi exercere pastoris officium."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p86","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Wherefore, for the solicitude of the said pastoral office, since we have perceived that ecclesiastical discipline, and the rule of living well and rightly through almost all grades of Christ's faithful, has so fallen, flowed away, and deviated further from the right path on account of the difficulty of the times, the malice, license, and impunity of men for the worse, that unless it be corrected with provident emendation, it is to be feared that it will daily more fall into various errors by the security of sinning, and soon burst forth with publicly arisen scandals.","Quare pro dicti pastoralis officii solicitudine cum ecclesiasticam disciplinam ac per omnes fere christi fidelium gradus bene recte que vivendi regulam temporum difficultate ac hominum malitia licentia que et impunitate in deterius ita labi diffluere aberrare que longius a recta via animadvertimus ut nisi provida corrigatur emendatione in varios errores securitate peccandi quotidie magis incidere mox que obortis publice scandalis prorumpere sit verendum."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p87","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Desiring, therefore, as far as is permitted to us from on high, to correct these already too much prevailing evils and to reduce many things to the pristine observance of the sacred canons, and to reform them for the better according to the institutions of the holy fathers, the Lord giving, and to moderate all things healthfully with the sacred Lateran council approving—indicted for this cause among others by our predecessor Pope Julius II of happy memory and continued by us—so that we may begin from those things which now seem more expedient and which, neglected through each age, have brought great ruin to the Christian religion and have produced the greatest scandals in the church of God, we have determined to begin from promotions to ecclesiastical dignities.","Cupientes igitur quatenus nobis ex alto permittitur ea iam nimium invalescentia mala corrigere ac pleraque in pristinam sacrorum canonum observantiam reducere ac in melius iuxta sanctorum patrum instituta dante domino reformare ea que omnia sacro approbante Lateranensi concilio a felicis recordationis Iulio papa secundo praedecessore nostro inter alia propterea incepto et per nos continuato salubriter moderari ut ab his sumamus exordium quae pro nunc magis expedire videntur et quae saepe per singulas aetates neglecta magnam christianae religioni attulere perniciem et in ecclesia dei maxima scandala pepererunt a promotionibus scilicet ad ecclesiasticas dignitates constituimus exordiri."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p88","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Since our predecessor Pope Alexander III of pious memory also decreed in the Lateran council that age, gravity of morals, and knowledge of letters be diligently inquired into in persons to be promoted to bishops and abbots, and that nothing more hinders the church of God than when unworthy prelates are assumed to the governance of churches—therefore, in the promotions of prelates, of which a great account ought to be taken by the Roman pontiffs, especially because they are about to render an account to God concerning those promoted by them to churches or monasteries on the day of the last judgment, we decree and ordain that henceforth, for all future times, to patriarchal, metropolitan, and cathedral churches and monasteries vacant for the time, provision be made of persons according to the constitution of the aforesaid Alexander—of mature age, gravity of morals, and the aforesaid knowledge of letters—not at the instance of anyone by commendam and administration or conservation or any other mode, unless on account of the utility of the churches, prudence, nobility, probity, experience, and ancient courtliness with competent literature and merits in the apostolic see, it shall have seemed otherwise to be done.","Cum piae memoriae Alexander papa iii praedecessor noster etiam in Lateranensi concilio decreverit ut aetas morum gravitas ac literarum scientia in personis promovendis in episcopos et abbates diligenter inquirantur nihil que magis dei ecclesiae officiat quam cum immeriti assumuntur praelati ad regimen ecclesiarum propterea in promotionibus praelatorum quarum a Romanis pontificibus magna ratio haberi debet eo praesertim quod de promotis per eos ad ecclesias seu monasteria in extremo iudicio rationem deo reddituri sunt statuimus et ordinamus ut deinceps perpetuis suis temporibus patriarchalibus metropolitanis ac cathedralibus ecclesiis et monasteriis pro tempore vacantibus de personis iuxta praefati Alexandri constitutionem aetate matura gravitate morum literarum que scientia praedictis non ad alicuius instantiam per commendam et administrationem seu conservationem aut alio quovis modo provideatur nisi ratione utilitatis ecclesiarum prudentiae nobilitatis probitatis experientiae atque curialitatis antiquae cum competenti literatura et in sede apostolica meritorum aliter visum fuerit faciendum."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p89","Session X (may 4, 1515)","We wish the same to be observed in elects and postulates whose elections and postulations are accustomed to be admitted by the apostolic see.","Idem que in electis et postulatis quorum electiones et postulationes per sedem apostolicam admitti consueverunt volumus observari."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p90","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And if it happen that provision be made for such churches or monasteries from persons younger than thirty years of age, let there be no dispensation with them that they may be able to be at the head of churches before the twenty-seventh year of age, but of monasteries before the twenty-second.","Et si de minoribus aetate triginta annorum ecclesiis aut monasteriis huiusmodi contigerit provideri non dispensetur cum eisdem ut ecclesiis citra vigesimumseptimum aetatis annum monasteriis vero citra vigesimumsecundum praeesse valeant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p91","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Nay rather, so that suitable persons may be promoted more accurately and more diligently, we decree that the cardinal to whom the report of the election, postulation, or provision of a church or monastery is committed, before he reports in the sacred consistory as is customary, shall make known the commission of examination and report of this kind given to him to the more ancient cardinal of each order in the consistory itself, through himself or, if the consistory shall not be on the day on which the burden of the commission was enjoined upon him, through his secretary or any of his domestic familiars; and they shall be bound to signify it to the other three prior cardinals of their order as soon as possible; and the said relator himself shall examine the business of the election, administration, postulation, or promotion summarily and plainly; and if any shall have contradicted, these having been called, suitable, grave, and trustworthy witnesses, and if expedient or necessary, others ex officio, he shall assume, and he shall bring the processes and rights of such report together with the statements of the witnesses on the day of the report to the consistory; nor shall he report in any way if the one to be promoted, present in the curia, shall not have visited the greater part of the cardinals beforehand, so that the cardinals may be able to know by ocular faith, as far as pertains to the person of the one to be promoted, what they are about to hear from the colleague relator.","Quinimo ut accuratius diligentius que idoneae personae promoveantur statuimus ut cardinalis cui electionis postulationis aut provisionis ecclesiae seu monasterii relatio committetur antequam in sacro consistorio ut moris est referat commissionem examinis ac relationis huiusmodi sibi datam uni cuiuslibet ordinis antiquiori cardinali in ipso consistorio per se ipsum vel si ea die qua sibi commissionis onus iniunctum fuerit consistorium non fuerit per secretarium suum aut alium quemlibet ex suis domesticis familiaribus notam illam facere debeat qui tres priores aliis quamprimum sui ordinis cardinalibus eam significare teneantur negotium que electionis administrationis postulationis ve aut promotionis summarie et de plano per se ipsum dictus relator examinet et si qui contradixerint his vocatis idoneos graves et fide dignos testes et si expediens opus ve fuerit alios ex officio assumere processus que et iura eiusmodi relationis una cum dictis testium die faciendae relationis se cum ad consistorium deferre debeat neque ullo modo referat si praesens in curia promovendus maiorem cardinalium partem antea non adiverit ut quae a referente collega sint cardinales audituri oculata fide quantum ad personam promovendi attinet cognoscere possint."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p92","Session X (may 4, 1515)","The one promoted, moreover, shall be held to visit those cardinals who were present in the curia as soon as possible according to ancient custom and laudable custom.","Promotus vero eosdem cardinales qui praesentes in curia fuerint ex antiquo more consuetudine que laudabili quamprimum visitare teneatur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p93","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Which custom and laudable custom we renew and command to be observed inviolably.","Quem quidem morem laudabilem que consuetudinem innovamus ac inviolabiliter observari mandamus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p94","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And since it is fitting that the episcopal dignity be preserved unharmed and that it be not exposed to the impugnations of the wicked or the calumnies of accusers, we decree that no bishop or abbot, at the instance and request of anyone, be able to be deprived of his dignity unless the opportunity and faculty of legitimate defense be permitted to him, even if the crimes be notorious, and the parties having been diligently heard and the cause fully proved; nor let any prelate be transferred unwillingly except for other just, efficacious, and reasonable causes, according to the form and decree of the Council of Constance.","Et quoniam episcopalem dignitatem tueri illaesam et ne improborum impugnationibus aut accusantium calumniis passim pateat muniri par est statuimus ut nullus episcopus aut abbas quovis instante ac requirente nisi sibi copia et facultas legitimae defensionis permittatur etiam si crimina fuerint notoria diligenter que partibus auditis causa plenarie probata fuerit dignitate privari valeat neve aliquis praelatus invitus nisi aliis iustis efficacibus que rationibus et causis transferatur iuxta formam ac decretum concilii Constantiensis."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p95","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And since from commendams of monasteries, as the mistress of experience has often taught, the monasteries themselves are gravely injured both in spirituals and in temporals—for their buildings partly collapse from the negligence of the commendataries, partly from their avarice or carelessness, and from day to day divine worship in them is more diminished, and matter for detraction is everywhere provided to persons especially secular, not without diminution of the dignity of the apostolic see from which such commendams proceed—so that their indemnity may be more healthfully provided for, we wish and decree that when they become vacant by the death of the abbots who presided over them, they may by no means be commended to anyone unless, for the preservation of the authority of the apostolic see and to meet the malice of those impugning it, it shall have seemed otherwise expedient to us, according to the quality of the times, by the counsel of our brothers.","Et quoniam ex commendis monasteriorum ut magistra rerum experientia saepius docuit monasteria ipsa tam in spiritualibus quam in temporalibus graviter laeduntur quippe quorum aedificia partim commendatariorum negligentia partim avaritia vel incuria collabuntur et in dies divinus cultus in eis magis diminuitur passim que obloquendi materia personis praesertim saecularibus praebetur non absque dignitatis apostolicae sedis diminutione a quo commendae huiusmodi proficiscuntur ut eorum indemnitati salubrius consulatur volumus ac sancimus ut cum illa per obitum abbatum qui illis praeerant vacaverint nullo pacto cuiquam possint commendari nisi pro conservatione auctoritatis apostolicae sedis et ad occurrendum malitiis illam impugnantium pro temporum qualitate aliter nobis de fratrum nostrorum consilio visum fuerit expedire."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p96","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But let provision be made for them of a suitable person according to the prescribed constitution, so that suitable abbots, as is fitting, are about to be at their head.","Sed de persona idonea iuxta praescriptam constitutionem eis ita provideatur ut illis idonei abbates prout decet praefuturi sint."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p97","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But those monasteries which have been commended—when such commendams have ceased by the cession or death of those to whom they were commended—may be commended only to cardinals and to qualified and well-deserving persons, yet in such a way that the commendataries of those monasteries to which they shall be commended thereafter—of whatever dignity, honor, and pre-eminence they may exist, even if they exercise the honor and dignity of cardinalate—if they have a separate table and apart from the conventual table, shall be held to impart the fourth part of their table for the restoration of the fabric or for buying, mending, and repairing ornaments, vestments, and paraments, or for the sustenance of the poor, as greater necessity shall require and persuade; but if the table be common, the third part of all the fruits of the said monastery commended to them, all other burdens having been deducted, for supporting the aforesaid burdens and the sustenance of the monks.","Ea vero monasteria quae commendata fuerint cum per eorum cessum vel decessum quibus erant commendata commendae huiusmodi cessaverint cardinalibus dumtaxat ac personis qualificatis et bene meritis commendari possint ita tamen quod eorum monasteriorum commendatarii quibus antea de cetero commendata fuerint cuiusvis dignitatis honoris et praeeminentiae existant etiamsi cardinalatus honore et dignitate fungantur si mensam habuerint separatam ac seorsum a mensa conventuali quartam suae mensae partem pro instauratione fabricae seu pro ornamentis vestibus ac paramentis emendis sarciendis que aut pauperum alimonia aut sustentatione ut maior exiget ac suadebit necessitas si vero mensa fuerit communis tertiam omnium fructuum dicti monasterii sibi commendati partem pro supradictis oneribus supportandis et sustentatione monachorum omnibus aliis deductis oneribus impartire teneantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p98","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And the letters which shall be expedited concerning commendams of this kind ought to be expedited with a clause specifically expressing this. Otherwise, if they be expedited otherwise, they shall be of no strength or moment.","Ac literae quae super monasteriorum huiusmodi commendis expedientur cum clausula hoc ipsum specifice exprimente debeant expediri. Alioquin si aliter expediantur nullius sint roboris vel momenti."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p99","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And since it is fitting that provision be made for such churches without any diminution of fruits, so that both the dignity of those presiding and the necessity of the churches and buildings may be provided for, we likewise decree and ordain that no pensions be reserved upon the fruits of the same churches unless by reason of resignation or also another [cause] which shall have been considered probable and honest in our secret consistory.","Et quoniam ecclesiis huiusmodi absque aliqua fructuum diminutione provideri decet ut tam dignitati praesidentium quam ecclesiarum et aedificiorum necessitati consulatur decernimus pariter ac statuimus ut super earumdem ecclesiarum fructibus pensiones minime reserventur nisi ex resignationis causa aut etiam alia quae in secreto nostro consistorio iuxta probabilis et honesta habita fuerit."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p100","Session X (may 4, 1515)","We also decree that henceforth parochial churches and greater and principal dignities and other ecclesiastical benefices whose fruits, rents, and proceeds do not constitute or attain the annual value of two hundred gold ducats of the camera according to common estimation, and also hospitals, leprosaries, xenodochia of whatever value, which have been instituted for the use and sustenance of the poor, be not commended to the cardinals of the holy Roman church nor conferred by any other title, unless they become vacant by the death of their familiars, which may be commended to them so that they be held to relinquish them within six months to the benefit of those persons pleasing to them and suitable; and we do not intend to prejudice them as to the benefices to which they would have regress.","Statuimus quoque ut de cetero parochiales ecclesiae ac dignitates maiores et principales alia que beneficia ecclesiastica quorum fructus reditus et proventus ducentorum ducatorum auri de camera secundum communem aestimationem valorem annuum non constituunt vel attingunt nec non hospitalia leprosariae xenodochia cuiuscumque valoris quae ad pauperum usum et alimoniam instituta sunt sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalibus non commendentur aut alio quovis titulo conferantur nisi per obitum familiarium suorum vacaverint quae illis commendari possint ut illa ad illarum personarum sibi gratarum et idonearum commodum infra sex menses dimittere teneantur quibus etiam quoad beneficia ad quae haberent regressum praeiudicare non intendimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p101","Session X (may 4, 1515)","We also ordain that the members of churches and of any monasteries and military orders be not disjoined or separated from their head—which is absurd—without legitimate and reasonable cause.","Ordinamus etiam ne ecclesiarum ac quorumcumque monasteriorum et militiarum membra a suo capite quod est absurdum absque legitima et rationabili causa disiungantur aut separentur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p102","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Perpetual unions shall by no means be made except in cases permitted by law or without reasonable cause.","Uniones perpetuae praeterquam in casibus a iure permissis vel sine rationabili causa nequaquam fiant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p103","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Dispensations, moreover, to several incompatible things beyond two shall not be granted unless to qualified persons according to the form of common law, except for a great and urgent cause.","Dispensationes autem ad plura incompassibilia ultra duo nisi qualificatis iuxta formam iuris communis non concedantur nisi ex magna et urgenti causa."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p104","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And for persons of whatever dignity obtaining parochial churches and their perpetual vicarages and greater and principal dignities even by way of union or commendam for life, to those obtaining beyond four, we from now on fix a term of two years, that in the meantime, retaining only four of them, they be held to relinquish the rest, and that benefices to be relinquished of this kind may be resigned into the hands of the ordinaries to the effect that provision be made for them by persons to be named by them, any reservation even general even resulting from the qualities of the resigning persons notwithstanding. Which term having elapsed, unless they shall have relinquished them, let them be considered to be all vacant and may be freely obtained as vacant, and such moreover retaining them incur the penalties of the extravagante \"Execrabilis\" of Pope John XXII of memorable memory, also our predecessor.","Et personis cuiuscumque dignitatis parochiales ecclesias et earum perpetuas vicarias ac dignitates maiores et principales etiam per viam unionis vel commendae ad vitam ultra quatuor obtinentibus exnunc terminum duorum annorum praefigimus ut interim quatuor tantum ex eis retentis reliquas dimittere teneantur et beneficia dimittenda huiusmodi resignari valeant in manibus ordinariorum ad effectum ut de illis provideatur personis per eos nominandis quacumque reservatione etiam generali etiam ex qualitatibus resignantium personarum resultante non obstante quo elapso termino nisi dimiserint omnia vacare censeantur ac possint libere ut vacantia impetrari tales que praeterea retinentes poenas extravagantis execrabilis recolendae memoriae Ioannis papae xxii etiam praedecessoris nostri incurrant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p105","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Likewise, we decree that special reservations of any benefices whatsoever be by no means granted at the instance of anyone.","Item statuimus ut speciales reservationes quorumcumque beneficiorum ad cuiusvis instantiam minime concedantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p106","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And since the cardinals of the holy Roman church precede all others in the same church after the supreme pontiff in honor and dignity, it is fitting and due that they shine before all with purity of life and splendor of virtues.","Et cum sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinales ceteros omnes in ipsa ecclesia post summum pontificem honore ac dignitate praecedant conveniens ac debitum est ut vitae munditia ac virtutum splendore cunctis praefulgeant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p107","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Wherefore we not only exhort and admonish them, but also decree and ordain that henceforth any cardinal for the time being, according to the doctrine of the apostle, live so soberly, chastely, and piously that, abstaining not only from evil but from every appearance of evil, he may shine before men.","Quapropter non modo hortamur eos ac monemur verum etiam statuimus et ordinamus ut de cetero quilibet cardinalium pro tempore existens iuxta doctrinam apostoli ita sobrie caste ac pie vivat ut non solum a malo sed ab omni etiam specie mali abstinens coram hominibus luceat."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p108","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Let him honor God first in his works.","Deum que in primis operibus honorificet."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p109","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Let all be watchful and intent upon divine offices and the celebration of masses, and have their chapels in an honest place, as they have been accustomed to do.","Sint omnes vigiles ac divinis officiis missarum que celebrationibus intenti habeant que capellas suas in loco honesto prout facere consueverunt."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p110","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Let their house, family, table, and furniture be not with pride or pomp nor with superfluous things nor in any way reprehensible, lest the license for sinning and exceeding the limit arise from it; but, as is equitable, let them deserve to be called a mirror of modesty and frugality.","Sit que eorum domus familia mensa supellex que non fastu aut pompa neque superfluis rebus neque aliquo modo reprehensibilis ne peccandi excedendi que modum licentia inde nascatur sed ut aequum est modestiae et frugalitatis speculum dici mereantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p111","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Let them, therefore, be content with those things which bespeak priestly modesty, and let them treat benignly and honorably, both publicly and privately, prelates and any other distinguished men coming to the Roman curia, and let them graciously and liberally undertake their affairs commended to us and our successors.","Illis itaque sint contenti quae sacerdotalem prae se ferant modestiam ac praelatos et quoscumque alios insignes viros ad Romanam curiam venientes benigne honorifice que tam publice quam privatim tractent et apud nos et successores nostros gratiose liberaliter que eorum negotia commendata suscipiant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p112","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Moreover, let them not have indecorous ministry of bishops and prelates in their houses, lest, having been instituted to preside over others and marked with the sacred character, they descend to vile ministries and everywhere induce contempt of the pastoral office.","Praeterea ministerium indecorum episcoporum praelatorum que in domibus non habeant ne ceteris praeesse instituti et sacro charactere insigniti in vilia descendant ministeria passim que pastoralis officii contemptum inducant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p113","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Let them therefore treat honorably, as brothers and according to the decency of their state, those whom they now have or will have in the future.","Eos itaque quos vel nunc habent vel in posterum sunt habituri honorifice ut fratres ac iuxta eorum status condecentiam tractent."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p114","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Since that the Roman pontiff is the common father of all Christ's faithful, it is very unfitting that those who assist him become acceptors of persons or advocates.","Cum que Romano pontifici communi omnium christi fidelium patri assistant personarum acceptatores vel advocatos eos fieri valde inconveniens est."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p115","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Therefore we decree that they not undertake any partiality, nor become promoters or defenders of kings, communities, or any others against anyone unless as much as justice and equity demand and their dignity and condition require; but, separated from all private passion, let them vacate with all diligence to calming and composing lawsuits among any persons, and let them promote with pious affection the affairs of kings and any others, and especially of the poor and religious, according to the business; let them aid the oppressed and unjustly burdened according to their strength and the duty of their office.","Propterea statuimus ne partialitatem suscipiant aliquam neque principum communitatum vel quorumcumque aliorum contra quemquam nisi quantum iustitia et aequitas postulat eorum que dignitas et conditio requirit promotores aut defensores fiant sed a privata omni passione seiuncti sedandis et componendis inter quoscumque litibus omni diligentia vacent incumbant que principum et quorumcumque aliorum ac praesertim pauperum et religiosorum iuxta negotia pio promoveant affectu oppressos et iniuste gravatos iuxta vires suas et officii debitum adiuvent."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p116","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Let them visit the places of their titles, if present in the curia, personally; if absent, through a suitable vicar, at least once each year. Let them inquire diligently concerning the clerics and peoples of the churches subject to their title; let them watch concerning divine worship and the goods of the said churches; let them explore skillfully the morals and especially the life of clerics and parishioners; and let them admonish all and singular with paternal affection to live rightly and honestly.","Tituli sui loca si praesentes in curia fuerint personaliter si vero absentes per vicarium idoneum semel saltem singulis annis visitent clericos et populos ecclesiarum titulo suo subiectarum cum diligentia inquirant vigilem que circa cultum divinum et bona dictarum ecclesiarum mores que in primis et vitam clericorum et parochianorum solerter explorent eos que omnes et singulos ad recte honeste que vivendum paterno moneant affectu."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p117","Session X (may 4, 1515)","For the increase of divine worship and the salvation of his soul, let any cardinal either give in life or leave in the article of death to his title as much as suffices to support one presbyter there conveniently; or if the church need repair or other assistance, let him leave or give to it as much as shall seem to his conscience.","Pro augmento autem divini cultus et salute animae suae quilibet cardinalis vel in vita donet vel in mortis articulo relinquat suo titulo quantum sit satis ad presbyterum unum commode ibi sustentandum vel si ecclesia indigeat reparatione vel alia subventione tantum illi relinquat vel donet quantum conscientiae suae videbitur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p118","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And since it is by no means fitting to neglect relatives and blood relations, especially well-deserving and in need of aid, but it is just and praiseworthy to provide for them, nevertheless we do not think it fitting that they be so filled either by the multitude of benefices or by ecclesiastical rents that others suffer loss from such intemperance of largition and scandal arise.","Et cum minime deceat affines et consanguineos praesertim benemeritos et ope indigentes negligere sed illis providere iustum et laudabile sit non propterea tamen vel beneficiorum multitudine vel ecclesiasticis redditibus ita eos impleri convenire arbitramur ut ceteri damnum ex huiusmodi largitionis intemperantia patiantur et inde scandalum nascatur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p119","Session X (may 4, 1515)","We therefore decree that they not rashly pour out the goods of churches, but expend them in pious and holy works, for the sake of which great and rich rents were decreed and ordained by the holy fathers.","Statuimus itaque ut ecclesiarum bona temere non effundant sed ea in piis et sanctis operibus exponant quorum causa magni et opimi redditus per sanctos patres statuti et ordinati fuerunt."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p120","Session X (may 4, 1515)","We wish also that for churches commended to the same cardinals, even if they be cathedrals, abbeys, or priories, or any other ecclesiastical benefices whatsoever, without all excuse, they take care and provide with all their effort that they be duly served; to cathedrals let them appoint worthy and suitable vicars or suffragans, according as the custom shall have been, with worthy and competent recompense; for other churches or monasteries commended to them, providing a sufficient number of clerics or chaplains or religious or monks serving God sufficiently and laudably; let them also preserve the buildings, possessions, and all rights in suitable state, and restore ruined ones, as pertains to the office of good prelates and commendataries.","Volumus etiam ut ecclesiis eisdem cardinalibus commendatis etiam si cathedrales abbatiales ve aut prioratus vel quaecumque alia beneficia ecclesiastica fuerint absque omni prorsus excusatione procurent ac omni conatu suo provideant debite inserviri cathedralibus dignos et idoneos vicarios seu suffraganeos prout consuetudo fuerit cum digna et competenti mercede apponentes reliquis autem sibi commendatis ecclesiis aut monasteriis de iusto clericorum vel capellanorum seu religiosorum aut monachorum deo sufficienter et laudabiliter servientium numero providentes aedificia quoque possessiones et iura quaecumque in statu convenienti conservent et diruta instaurent sicut ad bonorum praelatorum et commendatariorum officium spectat."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p121","Session X (may 4, 1515)","We also decree that the said cardinals use great circumspection and diligent providence concerning the number of familiars and horses to be fed at their own expense, lest having a greater number than their ability, condition, and dignity permits, they be able to be marked with the vice of luxury and prodigality, nor on the other hand be held avaricious and sordid if in great and ample rents they provide sustenance for very few, since the house of cardinals ought to be an open lodging, a port, and a refuge of good and learned men especially, and of poor nobles and honest persons.","Statuimus etiam ut dicti cardinales circa numerum familiarium equorum que suis impensis alendorum magna utantur circumspectione ac diligenti providentia ne maiorem numerum quam sua facultas conditiore ac dignitas patiatur habentes luxus ac prodigalitatis vitio notari possint neve rursus avari sordidi que habeantur si in magnis et amplis redditibus paucissimis victum praebeant cum domus cardinalium patens hospitium portus que ac refugium proborum et doctorum maxime virorum et pauperum nobilium honestarum que personarum esse debeat."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p122","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Let them therefore be prudent concerning the mode and number of keeping, and above all curious concerning the quality of familiars, lest they contract for themselves a foul mark of infamy from the vices of others and furnish just occasions to the people for speaking evil and calumniating.","Sint igitur circa modum et numerum tenendi prudentes ac de familiarium qualitate in primis curiosi ne ex alienis vitiis turpem sibi contrahant infamiae notam vulgu que obloquendi calumniandi que iustas praebeant occasiones."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p123","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And since it must be especially provided that our works be approved not only before God, to whom we ought first to please, but also before men, that we may be able to be an example to others to imitate, we ordain that any cardinal show himself to be the best rector and prefect of his house and family both concerning those things which are outwardly apparent to all and those which lie hidden within.","Et cum maxime providendum sit ut non solum coram deo cui primum placere debemus sed etiam coram hominibus opera nostra probentur ut ceteris exemplo ad imitandum possimus esse ordinamus ut quilibet cardinalis se domus ac familiae suae optimum rectorem praefectum que ostendat esse tam circa ea quae extrinseca omnibus apparent quam quae intus latent abscondita."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p124","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Let each of them therefore have priests and levites clothed in honest garments, and let him attentively provide that no one in his family, in any way a beneficiary and constituted in sacred orders, wear versicolored garments nor use that habit which little befits the ecclesiastical order.","Habeat itaque eorum quisque sacerdotes et levitas honestis vestimentis indutos attente que provideat ne quis in familia sua quoquo modo beneficiatus et sacris ordinibus constitutus vestes portet versicolores nec eo habitu utatur qui ordini ecclesiastico parum conveniat."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p125","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Wherefore, those constituted in the order of presbyterate ought to wear garments of colors which are not prohibited to clerics by law, let down at least to the ankles.","Quare in presbyteratus ordine constituti vestes colorum quae clericis a iure non prohibeantur deferre debeant usque ad talos saltem demissas."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p126","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And those having dignities in cathedrals and canons also of the same cathedrals and having the first dignity in collegiate churches, and the cardinal's chaplains celebrating masses, shall be bound to wear a hood in public; to squires, however, slightly above the ankles.","Et habentes dignitates in cathedralibus et canonici etiam dictarum cathedralium ac primam dignitatem in collegiatis habentes et cardinalium capellani missas celebrantes caputium deferre in publico teneantur scutiferis vero paulo supra talos concedantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p127","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Parafrenarii, because they are in constant motion and perform more laborious ministry, may use shorter and more expedited garments, even if they be clerics, provided they be not constituted in the order of presbyterate—yet in such a way that they do not depart from honesty, but so live that their morals do not differ from their ecclesiastical orders.","Parafrenarii quia in assiduo sunt motu ministerio que funguntur laboriosiore brevioribus ac magis expeditis vestibus uti possint etiam si fuerint clerici dummodo in presbyteratus ordine non sint constituti ita tamen ut ab honestate non discedant sed ita vivant ut mores ecclesiasticis suis ordinibus non discrepant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p128","Session X (may 4, 1515)","The remaining clerics, however, let them do all things temperately and modestly; and let not those clerics themselves, beneficiaries or constituted in sacred orders, nourish long hair nor beard, nor have mules or horses with phalerae and ornaments made of velvet or silk, but let them simply use such things of cloth only or leather.","Reliqui vero clerici temperate ac modeste omnia faciant et tam ipsi clerici beneficiati quam in sacris ordinibus constituti non comam neque barbam non nutriant neque mulas aut equos cum phaleris ornamentis que ex velluto aut serico factis habeant sed eiusmodi rebus ex panno tantum aut corio simpliciter utantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p129","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But if any of the aforesaid familiars shall have acted against this, or after three months, a legitimate monition having preceded, from the publication of the presents shall have worn such prohibited garments, let him incur excommunication.","Si quis autem familiarium praedictorum contra fecerit aut post tres menses legitima monitione praecedente a praesentium publicatione huiusmodi vestes prohibitas gestaverit in excommunicationem incurrat."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p130","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But if within another three months he shall not have corrected himself, let him be understood to be suspended from the perception of fruits from the benefices which he obtains.","Si vero infra tres alios menses non se correxerit a perceptione fructuum ex beneficiis quae obtinet suspensus intelligatur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p131","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But if he shall have persisted obstinately in such pertinacity for another six months, a legitimate monition likewise preceding, let him be deprived and be considered deprived of all benefices whatsoever he has, and those thus vacant may be freely obtained from the apostolic see.","Quod si per alios sex menses in huiusmodi pertinacia obstinatus permanserit legitima similiter monitione praecedente beneficiis omnibus quaecumque habet privatus sit et esse censeatur ea que sic vacantia a sede apostolica libere impetrari valeant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p132","Session X (may 4, 1515)","We wish all and singular these things to have place in our familiars and of any Roman pontiff for the time being, and likewise in all other clerics beneficiaries or constituted in sacred orders, and also curiales.","Quae omnia et singula in nostris et cuiuscumque Romani pontificis pro tempore existentis familiaribus et similiter in omnibus aliis clericis beneficiatis vel in sacris ordinibus constitutis ac etiam curialibus locum habere volumus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p133","Session X (may 4, 1515)","This one thing only excepted, that our said familiars and those of the Roman pontiff may wear garments of red color for the decency and custom of pontifical dignity.","Hoc uno tantum excepto quod dicti nostri et Romani pontificis familiares rubri coloris vestimenta gestare possint pro decentia et consuetudine dignitatis pontificalis."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p134","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And since it pertains especially to the cardinals to take care of the best works, they will labor according to their strength to know which regions are infected with heresies, errors, and superstitions against the true and orthodox faith, and where the ecclesiastical discipline of the divine mandates fails, and which kings or princes or peoples are infested with wars or fear to be infested. They will give effort to know these and suchlike, and to refer them to us and the Roman pontiff for the time being, so that opportune and salutary remedies may be able to be devised for such evils and pestilences with watchful study.","Et quoniam ad cardinales maxime spectat operum optimorum cura pro viribus laborabunt scire quae regiones haeresibus erroribus que ac superstitionibus contra veram et orthodoxam fidem infectae sint et ubi divinorum mandatorum ecclesiastica deficiat disciplina qui que reges ac principes seu populi bellis infestentur vel infestari timeant. Haec et huiusmodi scire ac nobis et Romano pontifici pro tempore existenti referre operam dabunt ut opportuna et salutaria talibus malis ac pestibus remedia vigilanti studio excogitari valeant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p135","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And since it is known by frequent and almost daily experience that many evils often happen to provinces and cities on account of the absence of their legates from the side, and various scandals arise not without prejudice to the apostolic see, we decree and ordain that it be lawful for no cardinals obtaining provinces and cities by title of legation to administer them through lieutenants or any officials, but they shall be held to be present personally for the greater part of the time, and to rule and govern them with all vigilance.","Et cum frequenti ac prope quotidiana experientia notum sit provinciis ac civitatibus propter suorum legatorum de latere absentium multa saepius contingere mala varia que non sine apostolicae sedis praeiudicio scandala oriri statuimus et ordinamus ut nullis cardinalibus provincias ac civitates legationis titulo obtinentibus eas per locum tenentes aut officiales quoscumque administrare liceat sed personaliter ipsi pro maiori parte temporis adesse atque eas omni vigilantia regere et gubernare teneantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p136","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And those who now obtain the title of legation or will obtain it for the time being, if in Italy, within three months; if outside Italy, within five months from the day of publication of the presents, shall be bound to go to their provinces and to reside there for the greater part of the time, unless by our mandate or that of our successors they be retained in the Roman curia for some more grave affairs or be sent to other places as necessity demands; and then in the said provinces and cities they shall have vice-legates, auditors, lieutenants, and the other customary officials with due provisions and salaries.","Et qui nunc titulum legationis obtinent aut pro tempore obtinebunt si in Italia intra tres menses si extra Italiam quinque a die praesentis publicationis ad suas provincias ire ac maiorem temporis partem ibi residere teneantur nisi de nostro et successorum nostrorum mandato pro aliquibus gravioribus negotiis in Romana curia retineantur vel ad alia loca prout necessitas postulat mittantur et tunc in dictis provinciis ac civitatibus vicelegatos auditores et locum tenentes ceteros que consuetos officiales cum debitis provisionibus ac salariis habeant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p137","Session X (may 4, 1515)","He who shall not have observed all and singular the premises shall lack whatever emoluments of the legation.","Qui praemissa omnia et singula non servaverit emolumentis quibuscumque legationis careat."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p138","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Which things were ordained and instituted for this purpose from ancient times, that the opportune presence of legates might be salutary to the peoples, not that they themselves, completely inexperienced in labors and cares, should gape only for profit under the title of legation.","Quae quidem propter hoc antiquitus ordinata et instituta fuerunt ut opportuna legatorum praesentia populis esset salutaris non ut ipsi laborum et curarum penitus expertes lucro tantum sub legationis titulo inhiarent."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p139","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And since the office of cardinal is especially concerned with frequent attendance on the Roman pontiff and the affairs of the apostolic see, therefore we decree that all cardinals reside in the Roman curia, and that those who are absent, if in Italy within six months, if outside Italy within a year from the day of publication of the present constitution, return.","Et cum cardinalis officium in primis versetur in frequenti Romani pontificis assistentia et sedis apostolicae negotiis propterea statuimus ut omnes cardinales in Romana curia resideant et qui sunt absentes si in Italia intra sex menses si extra Italiam infra annum a die publicationis praesentis constitutionis revertantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p140","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Otherwise, let them lose the fruits of benefices and the emoluments of all their offices, and let them altogether lack all privileges generally and specially granted to cardinals as long as they shall be absent—except, however, those whom it happens to be absent by reason of an office enjoined by the apostolic see or by mandate or license of the Roman pontiff, or by just fear, or any other legitimate cause of impediment or sickness, the privileges, indults, and immunities granted to the same cardinals and contained and expressed in our bull under the date of our coronation nonetheless remaining in their force.","Alioquin fructus beneficiorum et omnium officiorum suorum emolumenta amittant omnibus que privilegiis generaliter et specialiter cardinalibus concessis quam diu abfuerint omnino careant exceptis tamen illis quos ratione officii ab apostolica sede iniuncti vel de Romani pontificis mandato aut licentia vel iusto metu aut quavis alia legitima causa impediente vel aegrotationis abesse contingat privilegiis indultis et immunitatibus eisdem cardinalibus concessis et in bulla nostra sub data coronationis nostrae contentis et expressis in suo robore nihilo minus permanentibus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p141","Session X (may 4, 1515)","We also decree that the funeral expenses of cardinals, all things computed, ought not to exceed the sum of one thousand five hundred florins, unless the providence of the executors, just causes and reasons having been alleged, shall have judged that more must be expended.","Statuimus insuper ut expensae funebres cardinalium computatis omnibus mille quingentorum florenorum summam excedere non debeant nisi executorum providentia iustis allegatis causis ac rationibus plus expendendum esse duxerit."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p142","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Let the exequies and the castrum doloris be made on the first and ninth day; within the octave, however, let masses be celebrated according to custom.","Essequiae et castrum doloris prima et nona die fiant infra octavam vero missae de more celebrentur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p143","Session X (may 4, 1515)","For the reverence of the apostolic see, and for the utility and common honor of the pontiff and the cardinals themselves, so that the occasion of scandals which could arise may be removed, and greater liberty of votes may be made in the sacred senate, and it may be lawful, as is fitting, for any cardinal to say freely and with impunity whatever he feels according to God and his conscience—we decree that no cardinal reveal the votes given in the consistory and whatever things are done or said there which could redound to the hatred, prejudice, or scandal of anyone, in writing or by word or in any other way, under penalty of perjury and disobedience; and as often as by us and the Roman pontiff for the time being, beyond the premises, silence shall have been specially and expressly enjoined concerning any matter, if anyone shall have acted against this, beyond the said penalties, let him incur the sentence of excommunication latae sententiae, from which he cannot be absolved except by us or the aforesaid Roman pontiff and with expression of the cause, except in the article of death.","Pro reverentia autem sedis apostolicae utilitate que et honore communi pontificis et ipsorum cardinalium ut scandalorum quae nasci possent occasio tollatur maior que in sacro senatu libertas votorum fiat liceat que ut par est cardinali cuique secundum deum et conscientiam suam libere et impune quodcumque sentiat dicere statuimus ne quis cardinalium vota in consistorio data et quaecumque ibi gesta aut dicta quae in odium aut praeiudicium aut scandalum alicuius redundare possint scripto aut verbo vel quovis alio modo revelet sub poena periurii et inobedientiae et quotiescumque a nobis et Romano pontifice pro tempore existente specialiter et expresse ultra praemissa indictum fuerit super aliqua re silentium si quis contra fecerit ultra dictas poenas excommunicationem latae sententiae incurrat a qua non possit absolvi nisi a nobis vel praefato pontifice romano et cum expressione causae praeterquam in mortis articulo."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p144","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And since every age from adolescence is prone to evil, and to be accustomed from tender years to good is of great work and effect, we decree and ordain that masters of scholars and teachers ought not only to hear and instruct their boys or adolescents in grammar and rhetoric and the other such things, but also be held to teach those things which pertain to religion, such as the divine precepts, the articles of faith, sacred hymns and psalms, and the lives of saints; and on feast days they may be able to teach them nothing else than things pertaining to religion and good morals; and they shall be held to instruct, exhort, and compel them, as far as they can, so that they come to churches not only for masses but also for vespers and to hear divine offices, and likewise they impel them to listen to preachings and sermons; nor may they read to them anything against good morals or which leads to impiety.","Et cum omnis aetas ab adolescentia prona sit ad malum et a teneris assuefieri ad bonum magni sit operis et effectus statuimus et ordinamus ut magistri scholarium et praeceptores pueros suos sive adolescentes nedum in grammatica et rhetorica ac ceteris huiusmodi audire et instruere debeant verum etiam docere teneantur ea quae ad religionem pertinent ut sunt praecepta divina articuli fidei sacri hymni et psalmi ac sanctorum vitae diebus que festivis nihil aliud eos docere possint quam in rebus ad religionem et bonos mores pertinentibus eos que in illis instruere hortari et cogere in quantum possint teneantur ut nedum ad missas sed etiam ad vesperas divina que officia audienda ad ecclesias accedant et similiter ad praedicationes et sermones audiendos impellant nihil que contra bonos mores aut quod ad impietatem inducat eis legere possint."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p145","Session X (may 4, 1515)","For abolishing execrable blasphemy, which has grown beyond measure in great contempt of the divine name and of the saints, we decree and ordain that whoever shall have cursed God openly or publicly, or shall have blasphemed our Lord Jesus Christ or the glorious Virgin Mary His mother with contumelious and obscene words—if he bear a public office or jurisdiction, let him lose the emoluments of three months of the said office for the first and second time; if he shall have offended a third time, let him be thereby deprived of it.","Ad abolendam vero execrabilem blasphemiam quae in maximum divini nominis et sanctorum contemptum supra modum invaluit statuimus et ordinamus quicumque deo palam seu publice maledixerit contumeliosis que atque obscoenis verbis dominum nostrum Iesum christum vel gloriosam virginem Mariam eius genitricem expresse blasphemaverit si munus publicum iurisdictionem ve gesserit perdat emolumenta trium mensium pro prima et secunda vice dicti officii si tertio deliquerit illo eo ipso privatus existat."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p146","Session X (may 4, 1515)","If he be a cleric or priest, let him be fined for that very fact of having been convicted of such a delict, even of the fruits of any benefices he may have, to be applied, for one year; and let this be for the first time in which the blasphemer has so offended; for the second time, if he shall have so offended and been convicted as is preferred, if he have one benefice, let him be deprived of it; if, however, he have several, let him be compelled to lose that which the ordinary shall have chosen.","Si clericus vel sacerdos fuerit eo ipso quod de delicto huiusmodi fuerit convictus etiam beneficiorum quaecumque habuerit fructibus applicandis ut infra unius anni multetur et hoc sit pro prima vice qua blasphemus ita deliquerit pro secunda vero si ita deliquerit et convictus ut praefertur fuerit si unicum habuerit beneficium eo privetur si autem plura quod ordinarius maluerit id amittere cogatur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p147","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But if he be accused and convicted of his crime a third time, let him be thereby deprived of all dignities and benefices whatsoever he may have, and be rendered incapable of retaining them further, and they may be freely obtained and conferred.","Quod si tertio eius sceleris arguatur et convincatur dignitatibus ac beneficiis omnibus quaecumque habuerit eo ipso privatus existat ad ea que ulterius retinenda inhabilis reddatur ea que libere impetrari et conferri possint."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p148","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But a layperson blaspheming, if he be noble, let him be fined with a penalty of twenty-five ducats; and for the second time, fifty, to be applied to the fabric of the basilica of the Prince of the Apostles in the City, and to others as deduced below; for the third time, let him lose his nobility.","Laicus vero blasphemans si nobilis fuerit poena vigintiquinque ducatorum multetur et pro secunda vice quinquaginta fabricae basilicae principis apostolorum de Urbe applicandis et aliis ut infra deducitur pro tertia vero nobilitatem perdat."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p149","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But if he be ignoble and plebeian, let him be thrust into prison.","Si vero ignobilis ac plebeius fuerit in carcerem detrudatur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p150","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But if he be detected blaspheming publicly beyond two times, let him be compelled to stand with an infamous mitre for an entire day before the doors of the principal church.","Quod si ultra duas vices publice blasphemans deprehensus fuerit mitra infami per integram diem ante fores ecclesiae principalis mitratus stare cogatur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p151","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But if he shall have fallen more times into this same sin, let him be condemned to perpetual prisons or to the galleys, at the arbitration of the judge deputed.","Si vero pluries in hoc ipsum peccatum lapsus fuerit ad perpetuos carceres vel ad triremes damnetur ad iudicis deputati arbitrium."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p152","Session X (may 4, 1515)","In the forum of conscience, moreover, let no one guilty of blasphemy be able to be absolved without very grave penance to be enjoined at the discretion of a severe confessor.","In foro autem conscientiae nemo blasphemiae reus absque gravissima poenitentia severi confessoris arbitrio iniuncta possit absolvi."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p153","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But those who have blasphemed the other saints, we wish to be punished more mildly, at the arbitration of the judge, having regard to the persons.","Qui vero reliquos sanctos blasphemaverit arbitrio iudicis rationem personarum habituri mitius aliquanto puniri volumus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p154","Session X (may 4, 1515)","We also decree that secular judges who shall not have animadverted against those convicted of such blasphemy and shall not have affected them with just penalties—as far as in them lies—as if obnoxious to the same crime, be subjected to the same penalties.","Statuimus etiam ut saeculares iudices qui contra tales blasphemiae convictos non animadverterint eos que iustis poenis minime affecerint quantum in eis fuerit quasi eidem sceleri obnoxii eisdem quoque poenis subiiciantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p155","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But those who shall have been diligent and severe in inquiring into and punishing them, for each time let them obtain an indulgence of ten years and let them have the third part of the pecuniary fine.","Qui vero in illis inquirendis puniendis que diligentes et severi fuerint pro qualibet vice decem annorum indulgentiam consequantur et tertiam partem multae pecuniariae habeant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p156","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But whoever shall have heard a blasphemer, let him be bound to rebuke him sharply with words, if it can be done without his peril, and to denounce or notify him to the ecclesiastical or secular judge within three days.","Quicumque vero blasphemantem audierint eum verbis acriter obiurgare teneantur si citra periculum suum id fieri posse continget eumdem que deferre vel notificare apud iudicem ecclesiasticum seu saecularem intra triduum debeant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p157","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But if several shall have heard the said blasphemer at the same time, let each be held to accuse him, unless perhaps all shall have agreed that one should perform such office for all.","Quod si plures dictum blasphemantem simul audiverint singuli eum accusare teneantur nisi forte omnes convenerint ut unus pro cunctis tali fungatur officio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p158","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Whom all, in virtue of holy obedience, we exhort and admonish in the Lord, that for the reverence and honor of the divine name in their dominions and lands they command and cause all the premises to be kept and most exactly executed, about to have from God Himself the most abundant reward of so good and pious work, and about to obtain a similar indulgence of ten years from the apostolic see, with the third part of the fine with which the said blasphemer shall be punished, as often as they shall have taken care to punish such a crime.","Quos omnes in virtute sanctae obedientiae hortamur et monemus in domino ut pro divini nominis reverentia et honore in suis dominiis ac terris praemissa omnia servari et exactissime exequi mandent ac faciant uberrimam ab ipso deo tam boni ac pii operis mercedem habituri similem que annorum decem indulgentiam ab apostolica sede consecuturi cum tertia parte mulctae qua dictus blasphemus plectetur quoties tale scelus puniendum curaverint."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p159","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Which indulgence and the remaining third part of the fine we wish similarly to be granted and assigned to the accuser reporting the name of the blasphemer, other penalties against such blasphemers expressed by the sacred canons nonetheless remaining in their force.","Quam quidem indulgentiam et reliquam tertiam multae partem accusatori blasphemi nomen deferenti similiter concedi et assignari volumus aliis poenis contra huiusmodi blasphemos per sacros canones expressis nihilo minus in suo robore manentibus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p160","Session X (may 4, 1515)","So that clerics may live chastely and continently according to the precepts of the canons, we decree that those acting against this be sharply punished according to the canons.","Ut clerici praesertim caste continenter que iuxta canonum praecepta vivant statuimus ut contra facientes acriter secundum canones puniantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p161","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But if anyone, whether lay or cleric, be convicted of the crime on account of which the wrath of God came upon the sons of diffidence, let him be punished with the penalties imposed respectively by the sacred canons or civil law.","Si quis vero tam laicus quam clericus de crimine propter quod venit ira dei in filios diffidentiae convictus fuerit poenis per sacros canones aut ius civile respective impositis puniatur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p162","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But concubinaries, whether they be lay or cleric, let them be punished with the penalties of the same canons; nor let the tolerance of superiors or evil custom—which ought rather to be called corruption—on account of the multitude of sinners, or any other excuse avail them in any way, but let them be severely punished according to the censure of law.","Concubinarii autem sive laici sive clerici fuerint eorumdem canonum poenis multentur neque superiorum tolerantia seu prava consuetudo quae potius corruptela dicenda est a multitudine peccantium alia ve quaelibet excusatio eis aliquo modo suffragetur sed iuxta iuris censuram severe puniantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p163","Session X (may 4, 1515)","For the good and quiet governance of cities and all places subject to the Roman church, we renew the constitutions formerly issued by Aegidius, bishop of Sabina, of good memory, and we command and enjoin them to be observed inviolably.","Ad bonum vero et quietum regimen civitatum ac locorum omnium Romanae ecclesiae subiectorum constitutiones bonae memoriae Aegidii episcopi Sabinensis olim editas innovamus eas que inviolabiliter servari praecipimus et mandamus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p164","Session X (may 4, 1515)","And so that the stain and pest of nefarious simony may be cast out forever not only from the Roman curia but from every Christian dominion, we renew the constitutions issued by our predecessors also in sacred councils against such simoniacs, and we command them to be observed inviolably, and we wish the penalties contained in them to be held as expressed and inserted, and that delinquents be affected also by our authority.","Et ut nefariae simoniae labes ac pectis non solum a Romana curia sed ex omni etiam christiana ditione in perpetuum eiiciatur constitutiones per antecessores nostros etiam in sacris conciliis contra huiusmodi simoniacos editas innovamus eas que inviolabiliter servari praecipimus ac poenas in eis contentas pro expressis et insertis haberi et delinquentes etiam auctoritate nostra affici volumus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p165","Session X (may 4, 1515)","We also decree and ordain that anyone having a benefice with or without cure, if after six months from obtaining the benefice he shall not have said the divine office, a legitimate impediment ceasing, does not make his own the fruits of his benefices according to the rate of omission of recitation of office and of time, but shall be held to expend them as unjustly perceived on the fabrics of such benefices or on alms of the poor.","Statuimus quoque et ordinamus ut quilibet habens beneficium cum cura vel sine cura si post sex menses ab obtento beneficio divinum officium non dixerit legitimo impedimento cessante beneficiorum suorum fructus suos non faciat pro rata omissionis recitationis officii et temporis sed eos tamquam iniuste perceptos in fabricas huiusmodi beneficiorum vel pauperum eleemosynas erogare teneatur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p166","Session X (may 4, 1515)","But if beyond the said time he shall have contumaciously persisted in similar negligence, a legitimate monition having preceded, let him be deprived of the benefice itself, since the benefice is given for the office.","Si vero ultra dictum tempus in simili negligentia contumaciter permanserit legitima monitione praecedente beneficio ipso privetur cum propter officium detur beneficium."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p167","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Let him be understood, however, to omit the office in this respect, so that he can be deprived of the benefice, who shall not have said it at least twice within fifteen days; yet beyond the premises, about to render an account to God concerning such omission.","Intelligatur autem officium obmittere quoad hoc ut beneficio privari possit qui per quindecim dies illud bis saltem non dixerit deo tamen ultra praemissa de dicta omissione redditurus rationem."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p168","Session X (may 4, 1515)","Which penalty is to be reiterable as many times against those having several benefices as often as they are convicted of acting against [this].","Quae poena in habentibus plura beneficia reiterabilis toties sit quoties contra facere convincentur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p169","Session X (may 4, 1515) – Decree on the Printing of Books (inter Sollicitudines)","Leo, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Leo episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p170","Session X (may 4, 1515) – Decree on the Printing of Books (inter Sollicitudines)","Among the solicitudes lying upon our shoulders, we revolve with unceasing care that we may be able to reduce those who wander into the way of truth and to gain them for God, His grace cooperating with us. This is what indeed we seek with desire; to this we sedulously direct the affection of our mind, and we keep watch about it with studious diligence.","Inter sollicitudines nostris humeris incumbentes perpeti cura revolvimus ut errantes in viam veritatis reducere ipsos que lucrifacere deo sua nobis cooperante gratia valeamus. Hoc est quod profecto desideranter exquirimus ad id nostrae mentis sedulo destinamus affectum ac circa illud studiosa diligentia vigilamus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p171","Session X (may 4, 1515) – Decree on the Printing of Books (inter Sollicitudines)","Now, although skill in letters can be easily obtained through the reading of books, and the art of printing books—invented or augmented and refined especially in our times, with the divine favor assisting—has brought very many advantages to mortals, since with small expense a very great quantity of books is had, by which minds can be very conveniently exercised for the studies of letters, and learned men can easily become in every kind of language, and especially catholics, with whom we desire the holy Roman church to abound, who may also know and be able to instruct infidels in sacred institutions and salutarily to gather them into the assembly of the faithful through the doctrine of the Christian faith.","Sane licet litterarum peritia per librorum lectionem possit faciliter obtinere ac ars imprimendi libros temporibus potissimum nostris divino favente numine inventa seu aucta et perpolita plurima mortalibus attulerit commoda cum parva impensa copia librorum maxima habeatur quibus ingenia ad litterarum studia percommode exerceri et viri eruditi in omni linguarum genere praesertim autem catholici quibus sanctam Romanam ecclesiam abundare affectamus facile evadere possunt qui etiam infideles sciant et valeant sacris institutis instruere fidelium que collegio per doctrinam christianae fidei salubriter aggregare."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p172","Session X (may 4, 1515) – Decree on the Printing of Books (inter Sollicitudines)","Nevertheless, because the complaint of many has struck the hearing of us and the apostolic see that some masters of this art of printing, in various parts of the world, presume to print and publicly sell books—both those translated into Latin from the Greek, Hebrew, Arabic, and Chaldean languages, and others published in Latin and in the vernacular—containing errors also in faith and pernicious dogmas even contrary to the Christian religion and against the reputation of persons even shining with dignity, from the reading of which not only are the readers not edified, but rather they fall into the greatest errors both in faith and in life and morals, whence various scandals have often arisen—as the mistress of experience has taught—and greater are feared to arise from day to day; we, therefore, so that what was healthfully invented for the glory of God, the increase of faith, and the propagation of good arts be not turned into the contrary, and bring detriment to the salvation of Christ's faithful, have deemed that our care must be had concerning the printing of books, lest henceforth thorns grow together with good seeds or poisons be mingled with medicines.","Quia tamen multorum querela nostrum et sedis apostolicae pulsavit auditum quod nonnulli huius artis imprimendi magistri in diversis mundi partibus libros tam Graecae Hebraicae Arabicae et Chaldeae linguarum in latinum translatos quam alios latino ac vulgari sermone editos errores etiam in fide ac perniciosa dogmata etiam religioni christianae contraria ac contra famam personarum etiam dignitate fulgentium continentes imprimere ac publice vendere praesumunt ex quorum lectura non solum legentes non aedificantur sed in maximos potius tam in fide quam in vita et moribus prolabuntur errores unde varia saepe scandala prout experientia rerum magistra docuit exorta fuerunt et maiora in dies exoriri formidantur nos itaque ne id quod ad dei gloriam et fidei augmentum ac bonarum artium propagationem salubriter est inventum in contrarium convertatur ac christi fidelium saluti detrimentum pariat super librorum impressione curam nostram habendam fore duximus ne de cetero cum bonis seminibus spinae coalescant vel medicinis venena intermisceantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p173","Session X (may 4, 1515) – Decree on the Printing of Books (inter Sollicitudines)","Wishing, therefore, to provide opportune remedy concerning these things, with this sacred council approving, so that the business of printing books of this kind may prosper the more happily the more skillfully, diligently, and cautiously the search is henceforth applied, we decree and ordain that henceforth, for all future times, no one presume to print or cause to be printed any book or any other writing whatsoever, either in our City or in any other cities and dioceses, unless first in the City by our vicar and the master of the sacred palace, in other cities and dioceses by the bishop or another having skill in the knowledge of the book or writing to be printed, to be deputed for this by the same bishop, and by the inquisitor of heretical depravity of the city or diocese in which such printing would be done, it be diligently examined and approved by their subscription by their own hand, to be imposed freely and without delay under sentence of excommunication.","Volentes igitur de opportuno super his remedio providere hoc sacro approbante concilio ut negotium impressionis librorum huiusmodi eo prosperetur felicius quo deinceps indago solertior diligentius et cautius adhibeatur statuimus et ordinamus quod de cetero perpetuis futuris temporibus nullus librum aliquem seu aliam quamcumque scripturam tam in Urbe nostra quam aliis quibusvis civitatibus et dioecesibus imprimere seu imprimi facere praesumat nisi prius in urbe per vicarium nostrum et sacri palatii magistrum in aliis vero civitatibus et dioecesibus per episcopum vel alium habentem peritiam scientiae libri seu scripturae huiusmodi imprimendae ab eodem episcopo ad id deputandum ac inquisitorem haereticae pravitatis civitatis sive dioecesis in quibus librorum impressio huiusmodi fieret diligenter examinentur et per eorum manu propria subscriptionem sub excommunicationis sententia gratis et sine dilatione imponendam approbentur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p174","Session X (may 4, 1515) – Decree on the Printing of Books (inter Sollicitudines)","But whoever shall have presumed otherwise, besides the loss of the printed books and their public burning and the payment of one hundred ducats to the fabric of the basilica of the Prince of the Apostles in the City, without hope of remission, and the suspension of the exercise of printing for one continuous year, let him exist bound by the sentence of excommunication; and finally, if contumacy grow worse, let him be so chastised by his bishop or our vicar respectively, through all the remedies of law, that others, by his example, by no means presume to attempt similar things.","Qui autem secus praesumpserit ultra librorum impressorum amissionem et illorum publicam combustionem ac centum ducatorum fabricae basilicae principis apostolorum de urbe sine spe remissionis solutionem ac anni continui exercitii impressionis suspensionem excommunicationis sententia innodatus existat ac demum ingravescente contumacia taliter per episcopum suum vel vicarium nostrum respective per omnia iuris remedia castigetur quod alii eius exemplo similia minime attentare praesumant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p175","Session X (may 4, 1515) – Decree on the Printing of Books (inter Sollicitudines)","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p176","Session XI (december 19, 1516)","Leo, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for future remembrance of the matter.","Leo episcopus servus servorum dei ad futuram rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p177","Session XI (december 19, 1516)","Since among other things to be expedited in this sacred council, what was to be determined and declared concerning the sanction issued by certain prelates of the Gallic nation, both clerics and laypersons and also nobles and others favoring them, which they themselves call the Pragmatica [Sanction], would be expedient, and we most strongly desired to determine and declare it, just as also our predecessor Pope Julius II of happy memory, the inductor of this council, desired, and the prelates and other clerics and laypersons aforesaid had been many times cited to appear both before the aforesaid predecessor Julius and before us, and their contumacy had been often alleged or accused in the said council; and since afterwards, on the part of the prelates, clerics, and laypersons and also nobles and the aforesaid favorers legitimately called as is preferred, it was alleged that they had no place by which they would have to pass in order to be able to come safely to appear in the said council—we, lest under that pretext they might be able to excuse themselves in any way, took care to grant ample safe-conduct from the community of the Genoese, through whose places they might be able to come safely to the Roman curia to allege those things which they might wish to allege in such cause of the Pragmatica, and to be assigned to them.","Cum inter alia in hoc sacro concilio expedienda quid super sanctione per nonnullos praelatos Gallicae nationis tam clericos quam laicos etiam nobiles ac alios illis faventes quam ipsi pragmaticam vocant edita determinandum et declarandum foret expedire et declarare summopere desideraremus prout etiam felicis recordationis Iulius papa ii praedecessor noster huius concilii indictor desiderabat et praelati et alii clerici ac laici praefati ad comparendum tam coram praefato Iulio praedecessore quam nobis pluries citati fuerint et eorum contumacia in dicto concilio saepius allegata seu accusata extiterit cum que postmodum pro parte praelatorum clericorum et laicorum etiam nobilium ac fautorum praedictorum ad hoc ut praefertur legitime vocatorum allegatum fuerit nullum locum per quem transeundum esset ut tute venire ad comparendum in dicto concilio possent habere nos ne eo praetextu quoquo modo se excusare possent a communitate ianuensium per quorum loca tute ad Romanam curiam ad allegandum ea quae in huiusmodi causa pragmaticae allegare voluerint venire possint amplum salvumconductum concedi curavimus et eisdem assignari."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p178","Session XI (december 19, 1516)","And lest they be able to allege anything more against the premises and a just ignorance, to convince their contumacy, with the sacred council approving, we anew require and admonish the clerics and laypersons and also nobles, and also prelates and favorers, and the colleges of clerics and seculars, for a last and peremptory term, that they ought to appear legitimately before the forthcoming Kalends of October, all excuse and dilation ceasing; which term and dilation of this kind, for the aforesaid causes to remove all excuses, we prorogue, newly grant, and assign to the said day of the Kalends of October, as a last and peremptory dilation as is preferred.","Et ne aliquid amplius contra praemissa allegare et iustam possint praetendere ignorantiam clericos et laicos etiam nobiles etiam praelatos et fautores clericorum que ac saecularium collegia ad eorum contumaciam convincendam sacro approbante concilio de novo pro ultimo et peremptorio termino requirimus et monemus ut ante kalendas octobris proxime futuri omni excusatione et dilatione cessantibus legitime comparere debeant quem terminum et dilationem huiusmodi ex causis praedictis ad omnes excusationes tollendas ad dictam diem kalendarum octobris pro ultima et peremptoria dilatione ut praefertur prorogamus de novo concedimus et assignamus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p179","Session XI (december 19, 1516)","Which term having elapsed, we shall proceed to further things and the termination of the said negotiation, even by definitive sentence, their contumacy and non-appearance not obstructing, in the first next forthcoming session after that, which will be the eleventh session, on the 14th day of December after the feast of the blessed Lucy next to come, with the aforesaid sacred council approving, we indict.","Quo termino elapso ad ulteriora et terminationem dicti negotii etiam definitiva sententia procedetur eorum contumacia et non comparitione minime obstantibus in prima extunc futura sessione quam ad hoc et alia quamplura utilia peragenda videlicet undecimam sessionem quae erit die quartadecima decembris post festum beatae Luciae proxime futurum praefato sacro approbante concilio indicimus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p180","Session XI (december 19, 1516)","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p181","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","Leo, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Leo episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p182","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","By the provision of the supernal majesty, through whose ineffable providence heavenly things and earthly alike are directed, exercising the office of a watchman over the Lord's flock committed to us as far as is granted to our weakness, and revolving with ourselves with whole mind that among many other great matters it pertains to us also that the gift of preaching—which is principal and most necessary in the church of God, and of great fruit and utility provided it be exercised rightly and from sincere charity toward God and neighbor, and according to the precepts and examples of the holy fathers who, practicing these things, contributed very much to the church with the foundation and propagation of the faith—since our Redeemer first did and taught it, and by His precept and imitation that twelvefold number of apostles, as if so many declaring the glory of the true God of heaven, through the whole world, leading forth the whole human race which was held under the yoke of sin in ancient servitude from the darkness of their sense and exciting them to the light of perpetual salvation, propagated the word itself into all the earth and into the ends of the world—first they themselves, then their successors, far and wide, and most deeply founded it, so that those who now undertake so great a burden ought to be mindful and frequently revolve with themselves that they undergo and sustain the place of the author himself and founder, Jesus Christ, the most pious Redeemer, and also of Peter and Paul and the other apostles and disciples of the Lord in this office.","Supernae maiestatis praesidio per cuius ineffabilem providentiam caelestia simul et terrena diriguntur super gregem dominicum nobis commissum speculatoris officium quantum inbecillitati nostrae conceditur exercentes tota que mente nobis cum ipsi revolventes inter alia complura et magna ad nos etiam pertinere ut munus praedicationis quod in ecclesia dei praecipuum ac pernecessarium magni que fructus atque utilitatis dummodo recte et ex sincera caritate erga deum et proximum praeceptis que et exemplis sanctorum patrum qui haec profitentes cum fidei fundatione et propagatione plurimum ecclesiae contulerunt exerceatur. Quippe quod redemptor noster primus fecit et docuit et eius praecepto et imitatione duodenus ille apostolorum numerus quasi totidem caeli dei veri gloriam enarrantes per orbem terrarum universum genus humanum quod sub peccati iugo vetusta servitute continebatur sensum ab eorum tenebris educentes et ad perpetuae salutis lucem excitantes verbum ipsum in omnem terram et in fines orbis terrae tamen ipsi prius tum deinde sui successores longe late que propagaverunt altissime que fundarunt ut qui nunc tantum subeunt onus meminisse debeant creberrime que se cum revolvere praeter auctoris ipsius ac fundatoris Iesu christi pientissimi redemptoris Petri etiam et Pauli ceterorum que apostolorum et domini discipulorum se vices quoad hoc officium subire ac sustinere."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p183","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","Now, we have learned by the report of trustworthy persons that in our times—which we sorrowfully report—some preachers, not attending that they ought to exercise the office of those whom we have mentioned and moreover of the holy doctors of the church and others professing sacred theology who, assisting continually to Christians and opposing themselves to false prophets striving to subvert right faith, manifested the militant church to be such, unharmed by them, and that they ought to assume it only for meditating and perfecting things useful to the people attending their sermons for the extirpation of vices, the praise of virtues, and finally the salvation of the souls of the faithful—preach many and various things against the institutions and examples which we have mentioned, sometimes even scandalous to the peoples, which has moved our mind wonderfully, while we revolve with ourselves that they, unmindful of their office, in their sermons, laboring not for the utility of the hearers but rather for their own ostentation, flatter the vain ears of certain persons, who appear to have come to this point that the saying of the apostle writing to Timothy may be verified: \"For the time will come when they will not endure sound doctrine; but according to their own desires they will heap to themselves teachers, having itching ears, and will indeed turn away their hearing from the truth, but will be turned unto fables\"—the minds of which, false and vain, the preachers themselves not only do not study to lead back into the right and true way, but they involve them in even greater errors, while without any attestation or reverence of the canons, nay rather against the canonical sanctions, perverting the sense of sacred scripture in many ways, and rashly and wrongly interpreting it, preach against the truth, and threaten terrors and threats and many evils about to be near or already pressing, fortified by no legitimate reason but following only their own sense, and represent them as present, and assert them to be present, and often also thrust certain vain and empty things and other such things upon the peoples, and—which is more enormous—dare to assert that they have those things from the light of eternity and from the admonition or infusion of the Holy Spirit.","Sane fide dignorum relatione percepimus quod nonnulli praedicatores nostris temporibus quod dolenter referimus non attendentes se eorum quos diximus et insuper sanctorum ecclesiae doctorum aliorum que sacram theologiam profitentium qui assistendo iugiter christianis ac se falsis prophetis rectam fidem subvertere annitentibus opponentes ecclesiam militantem illaesam prius per se talem eam esse manifestarunt officium exercere et non nisi utilia concurrenti eorum sermonibus populo ad vitiorum extirpationem virtutum laudem animarum denique salutem fidelium meditanda et perficienda debere assumere multa tamen et varia contra institutiones et exempla quae diximus quandoque etiam scandalosa populis praedicare quod mentem certe nostram mirum in modum commovit dum nobis cum ipsi mente volvimus quod illi officii immemores sui in suis sermonibus non ad utilitatem audientium sed ad suam potius ostentationem laborantes vanis quorumdam auribus blandiuntur qui ad hoc iam devenisse apparent ut verificetur dictum apostoli ad Timotheum ita scribentis: erit enim tempus cum sanam doctrinam non sustinebunt sed ad sua desideria coacervabunt sibi magistros prurientes auribus et a veritate quidem auditum avertent ad fabulas autem convertentur quorum falsas et inanes mentes praedicantes ipsi praefati non modo in rectam veram que viam reducere minime student sed illas maioribus etiam erroribus implicant dum sine ulla canonum attestatione vel reverentia imo contra ipsas canonicas sanctiones sacrae scripturae sensum multifariam pervertentes temere que ac perperam plerumque interpretantes contra veritatem praedicare terrores que ac minas multa que mala prope diem affutura iam que ingruentia nulla prorsus legitima ratione muniti sed suo dumtaxat sensui obsequentes comminantur repraesentant adesse que asseverant plerumque etiam vana quaedam et inania et alia huiusmodi populis ingerere et quod enormius est ab aeternitatis lumine et sancti spiritus admonitione aut infusione illa se habere asserere audent."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p184","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","And since these persons, under the falsehoods of feigned miracles, disseminate various errors and frauds, finally, the peoples whom they ought sedulously to instruct in evangelical doctrine and to keep and preserve in true faith, drawing their sermons away from the sense and precepts of the universal church and deviating from the sacred constitutions which they ought especially to follow, they remove and make far from salvation.","Cum que ii sub confictorum miraculorum mendaciis varios errores fraudes que disseminant denique populos quos in evangelica doctrina sedulo instruere et in vera fide detinere et conservare deberent sermones suos a sensu et praeceptis universalis ecclesiae retrahentes a sacris que constitutionibus quas maxime sequi deberent deviantes auditores suos amovent ac longe faciunt a salute."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p185","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","For through these and other such things, simpler men, as more prone to deception, deviating from the way of salvation and obedience to the Roman church, are most easily led into various errors.","Per haec namque et alia huiusmodi simpliciores homines ut ad deceptionem procliviores a via salutis et obedientia Romanae ecclesiae deviantes in errores varios perfacile inducuntur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p186","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","Wherefore Gregory, most skilled in this office and inflamed with the fervor of charity, vehemently exhorts and warns preachers that they approach prudent and cautious about to speak to the people, lest, carried away by the impetuosity of speaking, they wound the hearts of hearers with errors of words as if with darts, and when they perhaps desire to be seen as wise, being deceived, they foolishly cut asunder the fabric of hoped-for virtue; for often the effect of words is lost, then the hearts of hearers are lightened by importunate or incautious loquacity.","Propterea Gregorius huius muneris peritissimus et fervore caritatis incensus praedicatores vehementer hortatur et monet ut dicturi ad populum prudentes cauti que accedant ne dicendi impetu rapti verborum erroribus quasi iaculis audientium corda confingant et cum sapientes fortasse videri desiderant decepti speratae virtutis compagem insipienter discindant saepe namque verborum effectus amittitur tum loquacitate importuna vel incauta audientium corda levigantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p187","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","And indeed, in no other thing do they affect the rude people with greater injury and scandal than when by preaching things to be kept silent, or by teaching false and useless things, they lead them into error.","Et sane in nullo alio rudem ii plebem maiori damno scandalo que afficiunt quam cum vel tacenda praedicant vel falsa et inutilia docendo ea in errorem inducunt."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p188","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","Which things, since they are known to be new and altogether alien and contrary to this sacred religion divinely instituted, are certainly worthy of grave and accurate examination, lest they produce destruction for the Christian people and for the souls of their authors and others.","Quae quoniam huic sacrae religioni divinitus institutae ut nova et aliena omnino contraria esse noscuntur. Gravi certe accurato que sunt examine digna ne scandalum populo christiano et suorum auctorum ac aliorum animabus interitum pariant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p189","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","We, therefore, to the holy church of God—over which we preside, the Lord disposing, which indeed is one and preaches and worships one God, firmly and sincerely confesses one faith according to the prophecy of the prophet: \"Who makes men of one manner to dwell in a house\"—wishing to reduce uniformity, postponement having been put aside, as much as with God we can, and to preserve it when retained, and desiring that those who preach the word of God to the people be such that the church of God may suffer no scandal from their preaching, and that if any be corrigible, they abstain in the future from those things which in our times they have presumed—since it is established that some of them, besides those things which we have said, in their preachings, not teaching the way of the Lord further in virtue, not explaining the gospel as they ought, but pressing and persuading everywhere with vast clamors feigned miracles and new and false prophecies and other light things little differing from old wives' fables, producing great scandal, having no regard for devotion and authority of that which disapproves and repels these things, and not sparing even those shining with pontifical dignity and other prelates of the church, to whom they ought rather to show honor and reverence, but have been accustomed to inveigh against their persons and state audaciously and rashly, and have committed other such things—so that so dangerous and contagious an evil and deadly pest may be radically extirpated and the deed so completely erased from the matter that not even the memory of it remain, with the sacred council approving, we decree and ordain that no one, whether secular cleric or regular of any order, even of the mendicants, or any other to whom the faculty of preaching pertains by law or custom or privilege or otherwise, be admitted to exercise such office unless he shall first have been diligently examined by his superior respectively—in which matter we burden the conscience of the superior himself—and be found apt and suitable for it by honesty of morals, age, doctrine, probity, prudence, and exemplarity of life; and wherever he shall afterwards come about to preach, let him legitimately prove concerning such examination and his suitability by authentic letters or otherwise to the bishops and other ordinaries of the places.","Nos igitur sanctae dei ecclesiae cui disponente domino praesidemus quae quidem una est et unum deum praedicat colit ad unam fidem firmiter et sincere confitetur iuxta illud prophetae vaticinium: qui habitare facit unius moris in domo uniformitatem quantum cum deo possumus posthabitam reducere et retentam conservare qui que populo verbum dei praedicant tales esse cupientes ut eorum praedicatione dei ecclesia nullum scandalum patiatur et si qui corrigibiles sunt ab his quae proximis nostris que temporibus praesumpserunt cum nonnullos eorum praeter illa quae diximus in praedicationibus non viam amplius domini in virtute docentes non evangelium ut deberent explanantes sed conficta miracula et nova ac falsa vaticinia alia que levia et ab anilibus fabulis parum distantia magnum que scandalum parientia nulla devotionis et auctoritatis eius que improbantis ac repellentis haec habita ratione vastis clamoribus imprimere ac suadere ubique conantes et ne pontificali quidem dignitate fulgentibus et aliis ecclesiae praelatis parcentibus quibus potius honorem ac reverentiam exhibere deberent sed in eorum personas ac statum audaciter ac temere invehi consuevisse et alia huiusmodi commisisse constat in futurum abstineant ut tam periculosum contagiosum que malum ac mortifera pestis radicitus evellatur et a re factum ita penitus deleatur ut nec memoria eius remaneat sacro approbante concilio statuimus et ordinamus ut nullus tam clericus saecularis quam cuiuscumque etiam mendicantium ordinis regularis aut quivis alius ad quem facultas praedicandi tam de iure quam de consuetudine vel privilegio aut alias pertinet ad huiusmodi officium exercendum admittatur nisi prius per superiorem suum respective diligenter examinatus in qua re conscientiam ipsius superioris oneramus ac morum honestate aetate doctrina probitate prudentia et vitae exemplaritate ad illud aptus et idoneus reperiatur et hic quocumque postea praedicaturus accesserit de huiusmodi examine et idoneitate sua per literas authenticas seu alias sui examinatoris approbatoris que episcopis et aliis locorum ordinariis fidem legitime faciat."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p190","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","We command all who sustain this burden and who will sustain it in the future that they preach and explain evangelical truth and sacred scripture according to the declaration, interpretation, and amplification of those [doctors] whom the church or long usage has approved and has hitherto received and will receive in the future for reading, and that they add nothing contrary or dissonant to their own sense, but always insist upon those things which do not disagree from the words of sacred scripture itself and the interpretations of the aforesaid doctors, rightly and sanely understood.","Mandantes omnibus qui hoc onus sustinent qui que in futurum sustinebunt ut evangelicam veritatem et sanctam scripturam iuxta declarationem interpretationem et ampliationem dictorum quos ecclesia vel usus diuturnus approbavit legendos que hactenus recepit et in posterum recipiet praedicent et explanent nec quidquam eius proprio sensui contrarium aut dissonum adiiciant sed illis semper insistant quae ab ipsius sacrae scripturae verbis et praefatorum doctorum interpretationibus rite et sane intellectis non discordant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p191","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","Let them by no means presume to preach the fixed time of future evils or the advent of Antichrist or the certain day of judgment, since the Truth says: \"It is not for you to know the times or moments which the Father has put in His own power\"; and it is established that those who have hitherto dared similar things have lied, and that the authority of the rest who preach rightly has been not a little detracted from on account of them—inhibiting all and singular the aforesaid secular and regular clerics and others, of whatever state, grade, and order they may exist, who assume this burden, that henceforth in their public sermons they by no means constantly predict any future things from sacred letters, nor affirm that they have had them from the Holy Spirit or divine revelation, nor assume to assert or treat in any other way empty and vain divinations, but according to the precept of the divine voice, let them set forth and declare the gospel to every creature with detestation of vices and commendation of virtues; and, everywhere fostering peace and mutual love so much commended by our Redeemer, let them not rend the seamless garment of Christ, but let them abstain from the scandalous detraction of bishops and prelates and other superiors and their state, whom they reprehend and bite before the crowd and laymen not only incautiously but intemperately, and from open and manifest refutation, sometimes by expressed names of evil deeds.","Tempus quoque praefixum futurorum malorum vel antichristi adventum aut certum diem iudicii praedicare vel asserere nequaquam praesumant cum veritas dicat non esse nostrum nosse tempora vel momenta quae pater posuit in sua potestate ipsos que qui hactenus similia asserere ausi sunt mentitos ac eorum causa reliquorum etiam recte praedicantium auctoritati non modicum detractum fuisse constet inhibentes omnibus et singulis clericis saecularibus vel regularibus praefatis ceteris que cuiuscumque status gradus et ordinis existant qui hoc onus assument ne de cetero in sermonibus suis publicis alia quaeque futura ex literis sacris constanter praedicere nec illa a spiritu sancto vel divina revelatione se habuisse affirmare et alienas inanes que divinationis asseveranda aut alio quocumque modo tractanda assumant sed ex divinae vocis praecepto evangelium omni creaturae cum vitiorum detestatione et virtutum commendatione enucleent et declarent et pacem ac dilectionem mutuam a redemptore nostro tantopere commendatam ubique foventes non scindant vestem inconsutilem christi sed ab episcoporum et praelatorum ac aliorum superiorum eorum que status scandalosa detractione quos coram vulgo et laicis non modo incaute sed etiam intemperanter reprehendunt et mordent et ab eis male gestorum expressis quandoque nominibus aperta et manifesta redargutione abstineant."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p192","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","Finally, we decree that the constitution of Pope Clement of happy memory which begins \"Religiosi\" be observed inviolably by them, which we renew and approve by the tenor of the presents, so that in this way, preaching for the utility of the people and gaining them for the Lord, they may merit to gain superabundantly the talent which they have received from Him, and to obtain His grace and glory.","Denique constitutionem felicis recordationis Clementis papae quae incipit: religiosi quam tenore praesentium innovamus et approbamus inviolabiliter ab eis observari debere decernimus ut hac ratione ad utilitatem populi praedicantes et eum domino lucrifacientes talentum quod ab illo acceperant superlucrari et eiusdem gratiam et gloriam consequi mereantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p193","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","Moreover, if the Lord shall have revealed certain future things in the church of God by some inspiration to some of them—as He Himself promises through Amos the prophet, and Paul the apostle, the prince of preachers, says: \"Do not extinguish the Spirit, do not despise prophecies\"—we by no means wish these to be counted among the herd of the fabulously false and others, or to be impeded otherwise.","Ceterum si quibusdam eorum dominus futura quaedam in dei ecclesia inspiratione quapiam revelaverit ut per Amos prophetam ipse promittit et Paulus apostolus praedicatorum princeps spiritum inquit nolite extinguere prophetias nolite spernere hos aliorum fabulosorum et mendacium gregi connumerari vel aliter impediri minime volumus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p194","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","For the grace of the Spirit itself is extinguished, as Ambrose testifies, if the fervor of the one beginning to speak is lulled by contradiction, and then injury is certainly said to be done to the Holy Spirit.","Extinguitur namque ipsius gratia spiritus Ambrosio teste si incipientibus loqui fervor contradictione sopitur et tunc spiritui sancto iniuria certe fieri dicitur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p195","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","And since it is a matter of great moment, because we ought not easily to believe every spirit, but test the spirits, as the apostle testifies, whether they be of God, we wish that by ordinary law such asserted inspirations, before they be published or preached to the people, be from now on understood to be reserved to the examination of the apostolic see.","Et quoniam res magni momenti est eo quod non de facile credendum sit omni spiritui sed sint probandi spiritus teste apostolo an ex deo proveniant volumus ut lege ordinaria tales assertae inspirationes antequam publicentur aut populo praedicentur exnunc apostolicae sedis examini reservatae intelligantur."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p196","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","But if it cannot be done without peril of delay, or urgent necessity persuades otherwise, then, the same order having been observed, let it be notified to the ordinary of the place, so that he, having associated with himself three or four learned and grave men and diligently examined such a matter with them, when they shall see it expedient—concerning which we burden their consciences—may be able to grant license.","Quod si sine morae periculo id fieri non valeret aut urgens necessitas aliud suaderet tunc eodem ordine servato ordinario loci notificetur ut ille adhibitis se cum tribus aut quatuor doctis et gravibus viris et huiusmodi negotio cum eis diligenter examinato quando id expedire videbunt super quo eorum conscientias oneramus licentiam concedere possint."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p197","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","But if anyone shall have dared to commit anything against any of the premises, beyond the penalties established by law against such persons, we wish them to incur also the sentence of excommunication, from which they cannot be absolved except by the Roman pontiff, except in the article of death.","Si qui autem contra premissorum aliquod committere quidquam ausi fuerint ultra poenas contra tales a iure statutas excommunicationis etiam sententiam a qua non nisi a Romano pontifice praeterquam in mortis articulo constituti absolvi possint eos incurrere volumus."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p198","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","And so that by their example others may by no means dare to attempt similar things, we decree that the office of preaching also be perpetually interdicted to them, notwithstanding constitutions, ordinances, privileges, indults, and apostolic letters granted to the aforesaid orders and persons—even those contained in the Mare Magnum, and even if perhaps approved, renewed, or newly granted by us—which we do not wish to avail them in any way in this respect.","Et ut eorum exemplo alii attentare similia minime audeant eis praedicationis etiam officium interdictum esse perpetuo decernimus non obstantibus constitutionibus ordinationibus ac privilegiis et indultis et litteris apostolicis ordinibus et personis praefatis etiam in Mari magno comprehensis ac etiam a nobis forsan approbatis innovatis vel etiam de novo concessis quae quo ad hoc nolumus eis in aliquo suffragari."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p199","Session XII (december 19, 1516) – Constitution Pastor Aeternus (condemnation of ","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p200","Final Session (march 16, 1517) – Constitution Divina Disponente Clementia (confi","Leo, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Leo episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p201","Final Session (march 16, 1517) – Constitution Divina Disponente Clementia (confi","With divine clemency disposing—by whom kings reign and princes rule—constituted on the eminent watchtower of the apostleship and over nations and kingdoms, although unworthily in merits, revolving in mind that even if those things which have been granted by our laudable and provident ordinance with our venerable brothers the cardinals of the holy Roman church for the salutary and quiet governance of kingdoms and for the perpetual stability of the same kingdoms, the peace and justice of peoples—especially to kings well deserving of the catholic faith and of the Christian republic and the apostolic see—obtain the full strength of firmness, we nevertheless sometimes, with the sacred council approving, add the strength of our innovation and approval, so that they may endure the more firmly inviolate the more often they shall be fortified by our authority and also by the protection of a general council; and we willingly apply effective work for their preservation, so that kings and the persons of their kingdoms, fully enjoying such concessions, privileges, statutes, and ordinances in the Lord, may rest in the sweetness of peace, tranquillity, and pleasantness, and more fervently persevere in their accustomed devotion toward the same see.","Divina disponente clementia per quam reges regnant et principes imperant in eminenti apostolatus specula et super gentes et regna meritis licet imparibus constituti animo revolventes quod etsi ea quae pro salubri et quieto regimine regnorum et ad pacem et iustitiam populorum perpetua eorumdem regnorum stabilitate regibus praesertim de fide catholica et de republica christiana et apostolica sede benemeritis laudabili et provida nostra ordinatione cum venerabilibus fratribus nostris sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalibus concessa ac ad effectum huiusmodi gesta statuta ordinata decreta facta ve sunt plenam roboris obtineant firmitatem illis nihilominus interdum sacro approbante concilio nostrae innovationis et approbationis robur adiicimus ut eo firmius illibata perdurent quo saepius erunt nostra auctoritate ac etiam generalis concilii praesidio communita efficacem que operam circa eorumdem conservationem libenter adhibemus ut reges et ipsorum regnorum personae concessionibus privilegiis statutis et ordinationibus huiusmodi in domino plene gaudentes in pacis et tranquillitatis ac amoenitatis dulcedine conquiescant et in eorum solita erga eamdem sedem devotione ferventius perseverent."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p202","Final Session (march 16, 1517) – Constitution Divina Disponente Clementia (confi","Recently, indeed, so that our spouse the church might be preserved in holy union, and that Christ's faithful might use only the sacred canons issued by the Roman pontiffs and sacred general councils, we, with the counsel of the same brothers and their unanimous consent, while we were at Bologna with our curia, decreed and ordained certain constitutions which henceforth, in place of the Pragmatic Sanction and the things contained in it, in the kingdom of France for the good of peace and concord and its common and public utility, were treated with our dearest son in Christ, Francis, the most Christian king of the French, and diligently examined with the same brothers, and agreed with the aforesaid king by their counsel, and accepted by the legitimate proctor of the same king—just as is more fully contained in our letters drawn up concerning this, the tenor of which follows and is such: \"That primitive church ...\"","Nuper siquidem ut ecclesia sponsa nostra in sancta unione conservaretur et per christi fideles sacris canonibus a Romanis pontificibus et sacris generalibus conciliis dumtaxat editis uterentur quasdam constitutiones quas de cetero loco pragmaticae sanctionis et contentorum in ea in regno Franciae pro bono pacis et concordiae ac illius communi et publica utilitate cum carissimo in christo filio nostro Francisco Francorum rege christianissimo dum Bononiae cum nostra curia essemus tractatas et cum eisdem fratribus nostris diligenter examinatas et de eorum consilio cum praefato rege concordatas et per ipsius regis legitimum procuratorem acceptatas de eorumdem fratrum consilio et unanimi consensu statuimus et ordinavimus prout in nostris literis desuper confectis plenius continetur quarum tenor sequitur et est talis: primitiva illa ecclesia ...."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p203","Final Session (march 16, 1517) – Constitution Divina Disponente Clementia (confi","We, therefore, so that the letters themselves—which were issued especially for this purpose, that in the mystical body, namely the holy church, perpetual charity and peace may endure inviolate, and if any members dissent, they may be conveniently reinserted into its body—may be observed the more as it shall be more clearly established that they have been approved and renewed by us with the said sacred Lateran council approving, with that mature and healthful deliberation which they were decreed and ordained, although for the subsistence and validity of the same letters they would need no other approval, yet for greater caution, so that they may be observed the more tenaciously and removed with more difficulty the more they shall have been fortified by the greater approval of so many fathers—with the sacred Lateran council approving, by apostolic authority and plenitude of power, we approve and renew the aforesaid letters with all and singular statutes, ordinances, decrees, definitions, pacts, conventions, promise, will, and penalties, inhibitions, and all and singular other clauses contained in them, especially that by which we willed that if the aforesaid king of the French should not approve and ratify the aforesaid letters and all and singular contained in them within six months from the date of the presents, and should not cause them to be accepted, read, published, sworn, and registered for all future times in his kingdom and other places and dominions of the said kingdom, by all prelates and other ecclesiastical persons and courts of parliaments, after the example of other royal constitutions, and should not transmit to us the patent letters or authentic writings of all and singular the aforesaid concerning the aforesaid acceptance, reading, publication, oath, and registration within the aforesaid time, or consign them to our nuncio existing with the king to be sent by him to us, and then each year should not cause them to be also read, just as the other constitutions and ordinances of the same king of the French which are in green observance, to be observed inviolably with effect—the letters themselves and whatever things followed from them would be null and void, of no strength or moment—we approve and renew them, and we command them to be observed and kept inviolably and irrefragably, and we decree and declare that they obtain the strength of perpetual firmness upon the event of the said approval and ratification and not otherwise or in any other way, and that all comprehended in the said letters are held and bound to the observance of the same letters and of all and singular expressed in them, under the censures, penalties, and other things contained in them, according to the tenor and form of the same letters, notwithstanding apostolic constitutions and ordinances and all those things which we wished not to stand in the way in the said letters, and any other contrary things whatsoever.","Nos igitur ut literae ipsae quae ob id praecipue editae fuerunt ut in corpore mystico sancta videlicet ecclesia perpetua caritas et pax inviolata perdurent et si qua membra dissentiant ea commode suo reinserantur corpori eo magis observentur quo clarius constiterit ipsas literas ea matura et salubri deliberatione ac nobis dicto sacro Lateranensi approbante concilio approbatas et innovatas quae statutae et ordinatae fuerunt licet ad earumdem literarum subsistentiam et validitatem alia approbatione non indigerent ad abundantiorem tamen cautelam ut eo tenacius observentur et difficilius tollantur quo tantorum patrum fuerint maiore approbatione munitae literas praedictas cum omnibus et singulis statutis ordinationibus decretis diffinitionibus pactis conventionibus promissione voluntate ac poenis inhibitione aliis que omnibus et singulis clausulis in eis contentis illa praesertim qua voluimus quod si praefatus Francorum rex supradictas literas ac omnia et singula in eis contenta intra sex menses a data praesentium computandos non approbaret et ratificaret et ea perpetuis futuris temporibus in regno suo et aliis locis et dominiis dicti regni per omnes praelatos et alias ecclesiasticas personas ac curias parlamentorum acceptari legi publicari iurari et registrari ad instar aliarum constitutionum regiarum et de acceptatione lectione publicatione iuramento et registratione praedictis intra praedictum tempus per omnium et singulorum supradictorum patentes literas aut authenticas scripturas nobis non trasmitteret aut nuntio nostro apud ipsum regem existente per eum ad nos destinandum non consignaret et deinde singulis annis etiam legi prout aliae ipsius Francorum regis constitutiones et ordinationes quae in viridi sunt observantia observari debent inviolabiliter observari cum effectu non fecerit literae ipsae et inde secuta quaecumque essent cassa et nulla nullius que roboris vel momenti sacro Lateranensi concilio approbante apostolica auctoritate et potestatis plenitudine approbamus et innovamus eas que inviolabiliter et irrefragabiliter observari et custodiri mandamus robur que perpetuae firmitatis in eventum dictarum approbationis et ratificationis et non aliter nec alio modo obtinere omnes que in dictis literis comprehensos ad ipsarum literarum ac omnium et singulorum in eis expressorum observationem sub censuris et poenis ac aliis in eis contentis iuxta earumdem literarum tenorem et formam teneri et obligatos esse decernimus et declaramus non obstantibus constitutionibus et ordinationibus apostolicis ac omnibus illis quae in dictis literis voluimus non obstare ceteris que contrariis quibuscumque."],["vol2/lateran-v/index.html","p204","Final Session (march 16, 1517) – Constitution Divina Disponente Clementia (confi","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli ergo .... Si quis autem ...."]];